window.__hvBdRegister("origen-against-celsus", {"key":"origen-against-celsus","sections":[{"i":0,"j":9,"loc":"Book I, Preface","title":"Why Origen answers Celsus at all","summary":"Origen opens with Jesus' silence before false witnesses and before Pilate: the true defence of Christianity is the lives of genuine disciples, not written argument. He tells his patron Ambrosius that a written apology may even weaken that defence, yet he agrees to answer Celsus' treatise, the so-called True Discourse, point by point. Paul's list of things that cannot separate believers from the love of Christ does not include argument, so no firm believer should be shaken. The book is written instead for outsiders and for those 'weak in the faith'. Origen explains that he wrote this preface after reaching the place where Celsus brings in his Jew, and apologizes for changing his plan of composition partway through.","ideas":["Christ's silence before accusers as the model: deeds refute slander better than words","The reply is aimed at the weak in faith and those outside it, not at mature believers","Celsus' arguments lack even the plausible 'vain deceit' of real philosophical sects","Origen first planned a summary of objections, then switched to close point-by-point refutation"],"terms":[{"t":"True Discourse (Alēthēs Logos)","d":"The title of Celsus' anti-Christian treatise, which Origen says no one advanced in philosophy would accept."},{"t":"apologia (defence)","d":"A formal defence speech; Origen fears a written one may weaken the defence that rests on facts."}]},{"i":10,"j":25,"loc":"Book I, Chapters 1–8","title":"First charges: secret societies, barbarian origins, sorcery","summary":"Origen takes Celsus' opening charges in order. Christians form illegal secret associations: Origen replies that conspiring against unholy 'Scythian' laws, as against a tyrant, is just. Christianity's Jewish root is barbarous: the Gospel has its own proof, the 'demonstration of the Spirit and of power', from prophecy and miracles. Christian morals are not new: God planted moral notions in all hearts, so agreement with Heraclitus or Zeno on images is expected. Christians do not use demon-names and spells but the name of Jesus, and their teaching is not secret, though like philosophy it has inner doctrines. Finally Origen accuses Celsus, an Epicurean, of hiding his views by speaking of a soul akin to God.","ideas":["Associations against unjust law are justified when formed for truth","Moral and anti-idol notions are written on every heart by God's finger","Exorcism works by the name of Jesus and the gospel narratives, not by incantations","Exoteric and esoteric teaching exists in philosophy and the mysteries too, so 'secrecy' is no reproach"],"terms":[{"t":"agapē (love-feast)","d":"The Christian common meal Celsus tries to discredit as a bond formed against common danger."},{"t":"apodeixis pneumatos kai dynameōs (demonstration of the Spirit and of power)","d":"Paul's phrase that Origen uses for the Gospel's own proof: fulfilled prophecy and miracles."},{"t":"exoteric / esoteric","d":"Doctrines taught openly versus those reserved for the prepared, as among the Pythagoreans."}]},{"i":26,"j":35,"loc":"Book I, Chapters 9–13","title":"Faith, reason, and the wisdom of this world","summary":"Celsus says one should follow reason, and mocks Christians who cry 'Do not examine, but believe!' Origen grants that Christianity contains much rational inquiry into prophecy and parable, but most people cannot leave daily life for philosophy; for them simple faith that reforms their lives is the best available remedy. Philosophers also choose a school on trust before hearing all the arguments, and all human enterprises such as sailing, marriage and farming rest on faith. Origen ridicules Celsus' boast to know all Christian doctrines, since no one claims to know all of Plato or the Stoics. Paul's 'wisdom of this world is foolishness' refers only to false philosophy, and Christianity prefers assent grounded in reason.","ideas":["Simple faith is a concession to the many who cannot study, not the Christian ideal","Choosing a philosophical sect is itself an act of belief","Celsus resembles a visitor to Egypt who learned only from the ignorant","Paul condemns 'the wisdom of this world', not wisdom without qualification"],"terms":[{"t":"pistis (faith)","d":"Belief without full demonstration; Origen defends it as practically necessary and present in every human undertaking."},{"t":"logos (reason)","d":"The rational guide Celsus demands; Origen says Christianity prefers reason where people can follow it."}]},{"i":36,"j":53,"loc":"Book I, Chapters 14–22","title":"The antiquity and wisdom of the Jews","summary":"Celsus lists ancient wise nations sharing a common doctrine but leaves out the Jews. Origen calls this arbitrary: he believes Egyptian and Assyrian histories yet rejects Jewish ones, while Numenius, Hermippus, Hecataeus, Josephus and Tatian credit the Jews with learning and age. Celsus praises Linus, Orpheus and others but omits Moses, whose laws a scattered nation still obeys. Origen compares Greek myths of gods castrating and warring with Moses' writings, which serve both simple readers and the wise. He rebuts Celsus' hints that the world is uncreated, that Egyptian animal-worship hides wisdom while Moses' laws do not, that Moses borrowed his doctrine, and that circumcision came from Egypt.","ideas":["Celsus believes other nations' histories but treats Jewish history alone as false","Moses' twofold writing serves the many morally and the few speculatively","Greek myths of divine crimes are worse than anything in Moses","Christian and Jewish doctrine keeps God unchangeable, unlike the Stoic bodily god"],"terms":[{"t":"allegory","d":"Reading a text for a hidden sense; Celsus allows it for Greek myths and Egyptian rites but not for Moses."},{"t":"ekpyrōsis (conflagration)","d":"The periodic world-burnings Celsus invokes alongside floods to suggest the world has no beginning."}]},{"i":54,"j":61,"loc":"Book I, Chapters 23–26","title":"One God, divine names, and Jesus' success","summary":"Celsus mocks the herdsmen who followed Moses into belief in one God. Origen demands that Celsus prove that Mnemosyne, Themis and the Graces really exist, and argues from the world's unified order to a single maker, who is neither a part nor even properly the whole. Against Celsus' claim that it makes no difference whether God is called Zeus or another name, Origen discusses the nature of names: powerful names lose their force in translation, so Christians die rather than call God Zeus, whose name brings his myths with it. He denies that Moses taught angel-worship or sorcery. He then argues that Jesus' worldwide success in a few years, and the moral reform of converts, could not occur without divine help.","ideas":["The world's harmony points to one maker, not many","Names are not arbitrary: Sabaoth, Adonai and the name of Jesus carry real power","Calling God 'Zeus' imports Zeus' parentage and crimes","Moral reform of many converts shows divine assistance"],"terms":[{"t":"Sabaoth / Adonai","d":"Hebrew divine names that Origen says belong to a secret theology of the Creator and have power when properly spoken."},{"t":"physei / thesei (by nature / by convention)","d":"The debate over whether names arise naturally or by agreement, which Origen cites from Aristotle, the Stoics and Epicurus."}]},{"i":62,"j":73,"loc":"Book I, Chapters 27–32","title":"Celsus' Jew and the slander of Jesus' birth","summary":"Origen notes that Jesus' teaching conquered princes, soldiers and cities, and that Celsus himself admits some intelligent converts. Celsus now introduces a Jew who tells Jesus he invented his virgin birth and was the son of a spinning woman driven out for adultery, who learned magic in Egypt. Origen argues that this humble origin makes Jesus' worldwide fame more wonderful, using Plato's story of Themistocles and the Seriphian. He asks why the disciples would risk death without belief in the resurrection, and compares Jesus' death to those who died to free a city of plague. He argues that the tale of a soldier Panthera unwittingly concedes that Joseph was not the father, and that by Platonic principles a soul of such merit deserved a noble birth.","ideas":["A man without birth, wealth or education who shook the world must be admired","Jesus is admired for wisdom, miracles and government together, despite the shame of crucifixion","One just man's voluntary death may drive off the evil spirits behind calamities","The Panthera story is a clumsy invention that still denies a normal birth"],"terms":[{"t":"Panthera","d":"The soldier whom Celsus' Jew names as Jesus' real father."},{"t":"metempsychosis","d":"The Pythagorean-Platonic idea that souls enter bodies according to past deeds, which Origen uses against Celsus."}]},{"i":74,"j":87,"loc":"Book I, Chapters 33–39","title":"The virgin birth and the prophets of Israel","summary":"Origen argues that souls receive bodies suited to them, so the soul of Jesus fittingly had a miraculous body, not one born of adultery. Celsus' Jew fails to cite Isaiah's prophecy of Immanuel; Origen defends the rendering 'virgin' for the Hebrew word from Deuteronomy and from the sense of a 'sign'. He argues that the Jews needed true prophets, even for small matters like Saul's lost asses, because they were forbidden pagan divination. To Greeks he points to vultures said to breed without males, to first men born from earth, and to the story that Plato was born of Apollo. He rejects the claim that Jesus was an Egyptian-trained magician and refuses to answer Celsus' coarse jokes about Mary.","ideas":["Bodies fit souls, as physiognomists hold, so a noble soul needs a pure birth","A 'sign' requires a virgin conceiving, not an ordinary young woman","God gave Israel prophets in place of forbidden pagan divination","A magician would not teach divine judgment or forbid magic"],"terms":[{"t":"Immanuel","d":"'God with us', the name in Isaiah's prophecy of the child born to a virgin."},{"t":"Olmah ('almah)","d":"The Hebrew word in Isaiah that the Septuagint renders 'virgin' and others 'young woman'."}]},{"i":88,"j":99,"loc":"Book I, Chapters 40–45","title":"The dove at the baptism and proving histories","summary":"Origen complains that Celsus attacks the Gospels in no order, jumping from the virgin birth to the baptism to the Magi, which ill suits his boastful title. Celsus' Jew asks what credible witness saw the dove or heard the voice from heaven. Origen first observes that proving any history is hard, as with the Trojan War and its myths, so readers must judge candidly what to accept literally or figuratively. He then argues that a Jew who believes the visions of Ezekiel and Isaiah cannot consistently reject Jesus' vision, especially since Jesus' works still convert people. He recalls a public debate in which he asked Jewish scholars to justify belief in Moses before demanding proof for Jesus.","ideas":["Celsus' disordered attack shows passion, not philosophy","Histories mixed with marvels require discernment, not blanket acceptance or rejection","A Jew who credits prophetic visions cannot reject the baptismal vision","The case for Moses and the case for Jesus stand or fall together"],"terms":[{"t":"logos sophias (word of wisdom)","d":"The spiritual gift by which Origen says one could explain why the Spirit appeared as a dove."}]},{"i":100,"j":105,"loc":"Book I, Chapters 46–48","title":"Living miracles, Josephus, and the divine senses","summary":"Origen says the miracles of Jesus and the apostles confirm the dove, and that Christians still expel demons, heal and foresee events; many are converted by visions or dreams. He cites Josephus' witness to John the Baptist and his claim that Jerusalem fell for the killing of James the Just, arguing it fell rather for the death of Jesus. He then explains the opening of the heavens: as dreams affect the mind without the bodily organs, prophets perceived through a divine sense with spiritual sight, hearing, taste, smell and touch, not by the heavens physically splitting. Jesus did not boast about himself, and Celsus' Jew wrongly links John's punishment with Jesus'.","ideas":["Ongoing exorcisms, cures and conversions are evidence of the Spirit","Josephus unwillingly comes near the truth about Jerusalem's fall","The prophets' visions were impressions on the mind, not physical events","A divine perception has five spiritual senses parallel to the bodily ones"],"terms":[{"t":"aisthēsis theia (divine perception)","d":"A higher faculty of sense that the blessed discover, perceiving incorporeal realities such as voices not in the air."},{"t":"James the Just","d":"Brother of Jesus, whose killing Josephus is said to have named as the cause of Jerusalem's destruction."}]},{"i":106,"j":115,"loc":"Book I, Chapters 49–53","title":"Prophecies of Christ: Bethlehem and Judah","summary":"Origen charges Celsus with ignoring the strongest evidence, that the prophets foretold Christ, and with giving his Jew words no Jew would use, such as a single prophet in Jerusalem announcing a 'Son of God'. Against the claim that the prophecies could fit many others, Origen gives examples. Micah's prophecy of the ruler from Bethlehem fits Jesus, whose birthplace cave and manger are still shown there, and was taught by the priests before his coming. He explains Jewish refusal of clear evidence by the power of habit and prejudice, as with Egyptians and their sacred animals. Jacob's blessing that rule would not fail from Judah until the expected one came is fulfilled in Christ, the hope of the Gentiles.","ideas":["Celsus' Jew speaks like a Samaritan or Sadducee, not a Jew","The cave at Bethlehem confirms the Gospel account of the birth","Prejudice explains why many Jews disregarded clear prophecies","Rule passing from Judah marks the coming of the 'expectation of the Gentiles'"],"terms":[{"t":"Christ of God","d":"The phrase Origen says Jews actually use for the coming one, rather than 'Son of God'."}]},{"i":116,"j":123,"loc":"Book I, Chapters 54–57","title":"The suffering servant and false messiahs","summary":"Celsus reproaches Jesus for his sufferings; Origen replies that they were foretold, quoting Isaiah's servant who bears our sins and is led like a sheep to slaughter. He recalls a debate with a Jewish scholar who read the passage as the people Israel in dispersion, and how he pressed him with the words that the servant dies for the iniquities of the people. Origen explains that the prophets speak of two advents, one in humility and one in glory, and cites Psalm 45, where God is anointed by God. To the claim that anyone born by providence is a son of God, he answers that Jesus is the source of all such sonship, and contrasts him with Theudas, Judas the Galilean, Dositheus and Simon Magus, whose movements vanished.","ideas":["Isaiah's suffering servant is an individual distinct from the people","Christ's two advents: suffering first, glory later","Psalm 45 speaks of God anointed by his God","Gamaliel's test: works of men come to nothing, works of God endure"],"terms":[{"t":"huiothesia (adoption)","d":"The sonship of those who do good for its own sake; Jesus is its source and beginning."},{"t":"Simon Magus","d":"The Samaritan magician whose followers, Origen says, number scarcely thirty in his day."}]},{"i":124,"j":131,"loc":"Book I, Chapters 58–61","title":"The Magi, the star, and Herod","summary":"Celsus' Jew confuses the Magi with Chaldeans and omits the star. Origen holds that the star was a new body like a comet, the kind said to appear at great changes, citing Chaeremon the Stoic and Balaam's prophecy of a star from Jacob. He suggests that the Magi, who worked through demons, found their spells failing when angels and Jesus' divinity broke the demons' power; consulting Balaam's prophecies, they came with gold for a king, myrrh for a mortal and incense for a god. Herod's attempt to kill the child shows the blindness of wickedness that tries to defeat fate. Jesus was a king, but not of this world, and avoiding danger until the proper time for a useful death is not cowardice.","ideas":["The star was a comet-like new body, foretold by Balaam","Jesus' birth weakened the demons on whom the Magi relied","The three gifts mark Jesus as king, mortal and god","Avoiding danger to serve others is not fear of death"],"terms":[{"t":"Magi","d":"Persian sages whom Origen distinguishes from Chaldean astrologers and describes as dealing with demons."}]},{"i":132,"j":141,"loc":"Book I, Chapters 62–66","title":"The apostles' character and the flight to Egypt","summary":"Celsus calls the apostles ten or eleven notorious tax-gatherers and sailors living by begging. Origen corrects the facts: twelve apostles, only Matthew a tax-gatherer, Peter and Andrew fishermen. Their success without rhetoric or learning proves a divine power. He admits from Barnabas, Luke and Paul that some were sinners, but argues that turning wicked men into models of virtue displays Jesus' power, as Phaedo and Polemo were converted to philosophy. Jesus' withdrawals from danger parallel Aristotle's retreat to Chalcis, and support from women followers matches philosophers' practice. On the flight to Egypt, Origen calls Jesus a compound being, divine and human, whose humanity was rightly guarded by ordinary means rather than public miracle.","ideas":["Unlettered apostles persuading nations shows divine power","Reforming sinners is a credit, as with Phaedo and Polemo","Prudent flight and accepting support are not disgraceful","The incarnate Jesus was guarded by human means, not spectacular rescue"],"terms":[{"t":"Epistle of Barnabas","d":"The 'general epistle' Origen cites as calling the apostles the worst sinners, perhaps Celsus' source."},{"t":"compound being","d":"Origen's term for Jesus as both divine Word and a man in a human body."}]},{"i":142,"j":151,"loc":"Book I, Chapters 67–71","title":"Miracles versus magic, and the body of a god","summary":"Celsus' Jew says that Perseus and Minos at least did great deeds, while Jesus gave no sign. Origen replies that the churches founded worldwide and the continuing power of Jesus' name are greater evidence. Celsus then grants the miracles but likens them to Egyptian jugglers in the marketplace; Origen answers that no juggler calls spectators to moral reform or lives a virtuous life. Against claims that a god's body would not be so born, fed or so voiced, Origen says Jesus took a real human body, ate and drank, and spoke with persuasive power, as the Pythian Apollo speaks through a priestess. God hates nothing he has made, and with Celsus' calling Jesus a sorcerer hated by God, Book I ends.","ideas":["Churches and the name of Jesus are present proof of his works","Miracles that lead to moral reform differ from jugglery","Jesus had a true human body capable of hunger, thirst and death","God hates nothing he has made"],"terms":[{"t":"goēs (sorcerer)","d":"Celsus' label for Jesus as a wonder-worker by magic, which Origen rejects."}]},{"i":152,"j":158,"loc":"Book II, Chapters 1–3","title":"Jewish converts have not abandoned the law","summary":"Book II answers the Jew's address to Jews who have become Christians, charging them with deserting the law of their fathers. Origen replies that Jewish believers, the Ebionites, still keep the law, and that Peter kept the food laws until his vision at Joppa and withdrew from Gentiles at Antioch; even Paul became a Jew to the Jews. Jesus' words that the disciples could not yet bear 'many things' meant the spiritual sense of the law, which the Spirit later taught Peter. Origen points to Paul's allegorical readings of Abraham's two sons and the muzzled ox as respect for the law. He notes that Celsus' Jew does at one point distinguish Christians who allegorize from those who keep the law.","ideas":["Jewish Christians keep the law, so the charge of desertion fails","Peter needed a vision to move from letter to spirit","Jesus withheld the law's spiritual meaning until after the resurrection","Paul's allegories honour the law rather than abandon it"],"terms":[{"t":"Ebionites","d":"Jewish believers in Jesus who keep the law; Origen derives the name from the Hebrew for 'poor'."},{"t":"letter / spirit","d":"The literal sense of the Mosaic law versus its spiritual meaning, to which Origen says believers ascend."}]},{"i":159,"j":168,"loc":"Book II, Chapters 4–8","title":"Gospel founded on the law; Jewish unbelief","summary":"The Jew asks why Christians start from Jewish worship and then despise it. Origen agrees the law and prophets are the entry to Christianity but says advanced Christians honour them more, finding mysteries that the Jews read as fables; Jesus said Moses wrote of him, and Mark begins with Isaiah. Christian teaching on resurrection and judgment is not stale, since the kingdom passed to the Gentiles. Even if Jesus kept every Jewish usage, he is still the Son of God, and he was not arrogant, as the washing of feet shows. To the Jew's question how those who foretold the Messiah could reject him, Origen cites human inconsistency and Isaiah's prophecy of seeing without perceiving, and points to the Jews' lost city, temple and prophecy.","ideas":["The law is the foundation of the Gospel, understood mystically","Jesus' meekness refutes the charge of arrogance","Isaiah foretold that the people would see and not perceive","Origen reads the loss of Jerusalem and temple as punishment"],"terms":[{"t":"mystical contemplation","d":"The spiritual reading of the law and prophets that Origen says educates the Church's mind."}]},{"i":169,"j":176,"loc":"Book II, Chapters IX–XII","title":"The Logos in Jesus and the betrayal by Judas","summary":"Celsus's Jew asks how one who fled, was captured and was betrayed by his own disciples could be a God. Origen answers that Christians do not call the visible body or soul of Jesus God, but the Logos who spoke in him, the Son through whom the Father created, who is not confined to that body. He denies that Jesus broke any promise, fled disgracefully or was taken against his will, citing the arrest in the garden. Judas, he argues, kept a remnant of respect and repented, which shows the power of Jesus' teaching; and a pupil's betrayal no more refutes Jesus than Aristotle's break with Plato refutes Plato.","ideas":["The Logos, not the visible body, is called God in Jesus","Jesus gave himself up voluntarily rather than being captured","Judas's repentance shows his apostasy was incomplete","A disciple's defection does not disprove a teacher (Plato and Aristotle, Cleanthes and Chrysippus)"],"terms":[{"t":"Logos","d":"The Word of God, Son of the Father, whom Origen holds spoke and acted in the soul and body of Jesus."},{"t":"oikonomia (economy)","d":"The divine dispensation of the incarnation, after which Jesus' soul and body became one with the Logos."}]},{"i":177,"j":190,"loc":"Book II, Chapters XIII–XIX","title":"Foreknowledge, real suffering and the willing death","summary":"Celsus claims the disciples invented Jesus' foreknowledge to cover his failures. Origen points to fulfilled predictions: Christians brought before governors and kings, the Gospel preached worldwide, the fall of Jerusalem, with Phlegon also crediting Jesus with foreknowledge. He argues the evangelists' frankness about Peter's denial shows love of truth, insists the sufferings were real so that the resurrection is real, and cites Er the son of Armenius as a Greek story of return from death. Socrates, Leonidas and Paul show that the wise may knowingly meet death, and he rejects Celsus's claim that forewarned conspirators always desist.","ideas":["Christians alone are pressed by judges to recant and go free","Recording the disciples' failures argues against invention","Jesus' sufferings were real, not in appearance as some heretics say","Facing foreseen death is honourable, as with Socrates and Leonidas"],"terms":[{"t":"Docetism (suffering in appearance)","d":"The view of certain heretics, rejected by Origen, that Jesus only seemed to suffer."}]},{"i":191,"j":203,"loc":"Book II, Chapters XX–XXV","title":"Prediction is not cause; the prayer in Gethsemane","summary":"Celsus argues that if Jesus as God predicted the betrayal, God made his own table-companions wicked. Origen replies that foreknowledge does not cause an event: the future act gives occasion for the prediction, and Judas remained responsible, as Psalm 108 and the oracle to Laius both show. He refutes the Stoic 'idle argument' with the cases of the physician and begetting children, and notes that many have plotted against their hosts. Against the claim that Jesus' chosen sufferings could not be painful, he holds that a real human body truly suffered, and he defends the prayer to let the cup pass as showing both fleshly weakness and a willing spirit.","ideas":["Foreknowledge of a free act does not make it necessary","The 'idle argument' about fate is a sophism","Celsus quotes the cup prayer without its obedient conclusion","Some sayings belong to the firstborn of creation, others to the man"],"terms":[{"t":"argos logos (idle argument)","d":"The fatalist sophism that if an outcome is decreed, any means to it, such as calling a physician, is useless."}]},{"i":204,"j":221,"loc":"Book II, Chapters XXVI–XXXIV","title":"Alleged inventions, prophecy and the Roman peace","summary":"Origen answers a string of charges: the disciples could not both believe Jesus was God and knowingly invent; only Marcionites, Valentinians and perhaps Lucianists altered the Gospel; and a real Jew would not say the prophecies fit others better. He argues that the peace under Augustus's single empire prepared nations for a Gospel that forbids revenge. He denies that learned Jews accept the Logos as Son of God, dismisses the genealogy objection about Mary's lineage, and cites the earthquake, darkness and Phlegon's eclipse. Against the Bacchae comparison, he says Pilate's wife's dream and the scattering of the Jewish nation outweigh Pentheus's fate.","ideas":["Corruption of the Gospel by sects is no charge against Christianity","Augustus's monarchy smoothed the spread of the Gospel","Celsus believes the Gospels selectively, only when they serve accusation","The mockery narratives teach endurance, not shame"],"terms":[{"t":"Pax Romana","d":"The union of many peoples under Augustus, which Origen sees as providential for the apostles' mission."}]},{"i":222,"j":239,"loc":"Book II, Chapters XXXV–XLIII","title":"Signs at the cross and Jesus' moral purity","summary":"To the demand that Jesus avenge himself, Origen points to the eclipse and other miracles, and answers the mocking 'ichor' question with the blood and water from his side and the centurion's confession. The gall and vinegar fulfil Psalm 69. He blames the Jews for neither refuting nor accepting the prophetic proof, and denies that Jesus won no followers, since envy arose from the crowds. Poverty and the cross are not evils, as Socrates, Democritus, Crates and Diogenes show, and Celsus's view that labour is the worst evil is un-philosophical. Jesus also preached to disembodied souls in Hades.","ideas":["Blood and water from a dead body as a portent","The first advent was to teach, not to judge","Poverty and suffering are not moral evils","Jesus as soul converted souls in Hades"],"terms":[{"t":"ichor","d":"The Homeric fluid in the gods' veins, which Celsus mockingly asks about and Origen contrasts with Gospel blood and water."}]},{"i":240,"j":251,"loc":"Book II, Chapters XLIV–XLIX","title":"Robbers, deserting disciples, and miracles versus sorcery","summary":"Celsus compares Jesus to condemned robbers; Origen replies that no sorcerer turned souls from sin, and the crucifixion between robbers was itself foretold. He grants the disciples' early cowardice but cites their later martyrdoms, and corrects the claim of only ten worthless followers with the five thousand and four thousand. Christians do not believe because Jesus was punished, but on many grounds, including the miracles foretold by Isaiah, the few raisings of the dead, and the greater spiritual healings worked by the disciples. Celsus says Jesus admitted that wicked sorcerers would work similar miracles; Origen answers that Jesus warned of false Christs, not sorcerers.","ideas":["Persecuted Christians resemble robbers only in their condemnation","The small number of recorded raisings argues against fiction","Spiritual healing of blind and lame souls exceeds bodily miracles","Jesus warned against false Christs, not rival magicians"],"terms":[]},{"i":252,"j":261,"loc":"Book II, Chapters L–LIV","title":"Divine power, lying wonders and the parallel of Moses","summary":"Origen cites Paul on the man of sin and his lying wonders and compares Moses with Pharaoh's magicians: the fruit of a miracle distinguishes divine from magical works. He lays down that where sorcery exists there must be genuine divine power too, and that wonder-workers must be tested by their lives and the moral results of their works, as with the nations founded by Moses and Jesus. Turning to the Jew, he argues that every objection to Jesus' miracles could equally be made against Moses, including Deuteronomy's warning about false prophets, and even the account of Moses' unknown grave.","ideas":["Evil counterfeits imply the existence of the genuine good","Miracles are judged by the reform of life they produce","Jesus founded a greater commonwealth than Moses","The Jew's arguments recoil upon Moses"],"terms":[{"t":"antichrist","d":"The 'man of sin' of 2 Thessalonians and Daniel, whose signs Origen calls lying wonders."}]},{"i":262,"j":278,"loc":"Book II, Chapters LV–LXII","title":"The resurrection against tales of Hades","summary":"Celsus's Jew compares the resurrection to tales of Zamolxis, Pythagoras, Rhampsinitus, Orpheus and others, and says only a half-frantic woman and deluded companions saw the risen Jesus. Origen turns the charges back on Moses, and argues that Jesus' public crucifixion ruled out a hidden withdrawal like the heroes', while the disciples' readiness to die proves sincerity. A Jew who believes Elijah and Elisha raised children cannot deny bodily resurrection. Celsus's dream-and-phantom theory, Origen says, would at least prove the soul's survival, and Thomas's test shows the risen body was real, though intermediate between earthly flesh and a bare soul.","ideas":["Public death excludes the trick of feigned disappearance","Jesus was raised by the Father, not by a prophet","Waking visions in the sane are implausible","The risen body is intermediate between flesh and soul"],"terms":[{"t":"antitype body","d":"The resurrection body corresponding to the former one, which Thomas doubted until he touched the wounds."}]},{"i":279,"j":292,"loc":"Book II, Chapters LXIII–LXIX","title":"Why the risen Jesus did not appear to all","summary":"Origen treats seriously Celsus's charge that Jesus should have shown himself to his judge, his tormentors and everyone. He argues that Jesus appeared differently to different people according to their capacity, as at the Transfiguration, and after he 'put off the governments and powers' the multitude could no longer see him; Paul's list of witnesses reflects this graded seeing. He spared his enemies, as the men of Sodom were struck blind. To the demand that he vanish from the cross, he replies that the events are symbols: crucifixion, death and burial in a new rock-hewn tomb signify the believer's dying with Christ.","ideas":["Jesus was 'more things than one' depending on the viewer","Divine glory is shown according to each one's capacity","He came both to be known and to be hidden","The new, single-rock tomb symbolises the purity of his burial"],"terms":[{"t":"epinoiai (aspects)","d":"The many titles of Christ (way, truth, bread, door) under which the one Jesus is regarded."}]},{"i":293,"j":305,"loc":"Book II, Chapters LXX–LXXV","title":"Hiddenness, the heavenly voice and Jewish unbelief","summary":"Origen denies that Jesus concealed himself, noting that he taught openly in the temple and appeared to both Marys, not one woman, and he exposes a contradiction in Celsus's 'the opposite ought to have happened.' Jesus taught of the Father, who leads the pious to light and punishes sinners. The voice at the baptism was not air struck but heard only by those meant to hear it, with a spiritual organ. To 'what God is disbelieved by those who await him?', Origen answers that Israel disbelieved even at Sinai and made the calf, so rejecting Jesus fits their history.","ideas":["Jesus' human traits were visible to all, his divine ones not","The divine voice is heard by a finer, spiritual hearing","Israel's unbelief in the desert parallels its rejection of Jesus"],"terms":[]},{"i":306,"j":313,"loc":"Book II, Chapters LXXVI–LXXIX","title":"Threats, resurrection hope and the end of Book II","summary":"Celsus says Jesus' 'Woe unto you' shows an inability to persuade. Origen answers that the prophets, Isaiah and Ezekiel, use the same threats, and that Celsus accepts Homer's Hermes rebuking Odysseus as healing correction. To the Jew's hope of a bodily resurrection through one sent by God, he points to Jesus as firstborn from the dead. Jesus did not come to produce unbelief, but used it to call the Gentiles, as Deuteronomy foretold. The Jew concludes Jesus was a mere man; Origen replies that no mere man could have converted so many, even the most passionate, to virtue, and closes Book II.","ideas":["Prophetic threats are medicine fitted to the hearer","Jewish unbelief served the calling of the Gentiles","The moral conversion of multitudes proves divine power","Christ was foretold by all the prophets, not one"],"terms":[]},{"i":314,"j":331,"loc":"Book III, Chapters I–VIII","title":"The ass's shadow and the charge of revolt","summary":"Opening Book III, Origen turns to Celsus's own arguments. Celsus calls the Jewish–Christian dispute over whether the Saviour has come a 'fight about the shadow of an ass'; Origen argues the prophecies are weighty and that Israel needed true prophets and miracles to keep it from foreign oracles and demons. Celsus says the Jews were rebel Egyptians and Christians in turn rebelled against the Jews. Origen refutes this from the Hebrew language and names, and argues Christians, forbidden to kill or resist persecutors, could not have originated in revolt; God limits their persecutions.","ideas":["The prophets spoke under divine influence; the dispute is not trivial","Israel's endurance under persecution argues for real prophecy","Hebrew speech and names disprove an Egyptian origin","Christian non-violence excludes origin in sedition"],"terms":[{"t":"onou skia (an ass's shadow)","d":"Proverb for a pointless quarrel, Celsus's term for the Jewish–Christian debate."}]},{"i":332,"j":357,"loc":"Book III, Chapters IX–XXI","title":"Mission, heresies and the Egyptian temple comparison","summary":"Origen refutes Celsus's claim that Christians would not want everyone converted, citing poor itinerant preachers, and shows early believers were neither few nor of one mind. Heresies, he argues, arise in every important field, such as medicine, philosophy and Judaism, and the wisest Christian studies them, though sects like the Ophites and Cainites are not truly Christian. Christian union rests on divine working, not fear or rebellion, and the only 'terror' taught is future judgment, which Celsus himself accepts. Against the comparison with Egyptian temples hiding animal gods, he appeals to Paul's wisdom spoken among the perfect and the inner meaning of the Gospel parables.","ideas":["Heresies reflect earnest inquiry, not mere faction","Christian unity rests on God's working","Future punishment is the only 'terror', and Celsus accepts it","Christianity has a deeper wisdom for the mature"],"terms":[{"t":"hairesis (heresy, school)","d":"A school of opinion; Origen notes medicine and philosophy have many."},{"t":"teleioi (the perfect)","d":"Advanced believers to whom Paul speaks hidden wisdom."}]},{"i":358,"j":366,"loc":"Book III, ch. 22–25","title":"Deified heroes, Aesculapius, and Apollo's oracle","summary":"Celsus contrasts the Christians' refusal to call the Dioscuri, Hercules, Aesculapius and Dionysus gods with their worship of Jesus, whom he says appeared after death only as a shadow. Origen answers that these figures are at most souls or heroes, and that the stories told of them (Hercules with Omphale, Aesculapius struck by lightning, Dionysus in female dress) show no virtue worthy of worship, while nothing licentious is recorded of Jesus. He compares the eye-witness testimony of the disciples, who suffered for it, with the claims about Aesculapius, and cites cures still worked in the name of Jesus. Even granting a healing spirit and Apollo's foreknowledge, he argues that healing and prediction are morally indifferent and prove no divinity, pointing to the Pythian oracle's honours for the boxer Cleomedes and the poet Archilochus.","ideas":["The myths of the deified heroes record vices that disqualify them from worship","Disciples who suffered for their testimony are more credible than tales of Aesculapius","Healing and foreknowledge are 'things indifferent' and can belong to the wicked","The Pythian oracle honoured a boxer and an obscene poet but not Socrates or Pythagoras"],"terms":[{"t":"adiaphoron (thing indifferent)","d":"A thing neither good nor bad in itself; Origen so classes bodily healing and knowledge of the future."},{"t":"heroes","d":"Souls of famous men thought to survive death; Origen allows these figures may be such, not gods."}]},{"i":367,"j":378,"loc":"Book III, ch. 26–31","title":"Aristeas, Abaris, and the churches of God","summary":"Celsus cites Aristeas of Proconnesus, who vanished and reappeared and whom Apollo told the Metapontines to honour, and Origen quotes Herodotus' account of him at length. Origen objects that Celsus accepts such marvels without proof while calling the Gospel miracles fictions, and asks what purpose of Providence or benefit to mankind the Aristeas story serves. By contrast the miracles of Jesus commended a saving doctrine, foretold by prophets, and began a union of divine and human nature that can make believers divine. The demons who feed on sacrifices opposed this teaching, yet God made churches spread everywhere; the churches of Athens, Corinth and Alexandria surpass the civic assemblies, and their councillors and rulers excel those of the cities. Abaris the Hyperborean, borne along like an arrow, is likewise dismissed as purposeless.","ideas":["A miracle is credible when it serves a worthy divine purpose and benefits mankind","Apollo is a demon seeking libations and sacrificial odours","The church in each city is morally superior to the city's own assembly","In Jesus the human nature began to be made divine through communion with the divine"],"terms":[{"t":"ekklēsia (church/assembly)","d":"Origen plays on the word for both the Christian church and the civic assembly of a Greek city."},{"t":"Providence","d":"God's purposeful ordering of events; Origen asks what end each reported marvel serves."}]},{"i":379,"j":390,"loc":"Book III, ch. 32–37","title":"The Clazomenian, Cleomedes, local cults, Antinous","summary":"Origen takes up further cases Celsus adduces: the Clazomenian whose soul left his body, Cleomedes of Astypalaea who vanished from a chest, and the cults of Zamolxis, Mopsus, Amphilochus, Amphiaraus and Trophonius. He suggests demons spread such stories to cheapen the accounts of Jesus, who laid down his life of his own power and took it again. He presses Celsus with a dilemma: either these cult figures are nothing, making him an Epicurean, or they are demons or heroes, in which case Jesus is at least such a being and mightier, since he alone forbids worship of the others. The honours paid to Hadrian's favourite Antinous are traced to Egyptian magic and demons; Christians, taught that the pagan gods are greedy demons, will not let Jesus be compared to Apollo or Zeus.","ideas":["Jesus laid down and took up his life by his own power","Celsus must either deny the cult figures' power (Epicureanism) or concede Jesus a higher one","Cult statues and temples suit demons bound to particular places","Antinous' reputed powers were produced by Egyptian magical arts after his death"],"terms":[{"t":"demons","d":"For Origen, earthly spirits that delight in sacrifice and pass themselves off as gods."},{"t":"High Priest","d":"Title Origen gives Jesus as the one who conveys prayers to the supreme God."}]},{"i":391,"j":403,"loc":"Book III, ch. 38–43","title":"Faith, the transformed body, and Zeus's tomb","summary":"Origen calls belief in Antinous unfortunate and belief in Jesus either fortunate or the fruit of investigation, and digresses on how upbringing and teachers shape belief, tracing such fortune to providence. He defends faith against Celsus' charge that it merely seizes the mind, citing the guileless sincerity of the Gospel writers and the agreement of Christian teaching with the common notions implanted at birth. To the charge of worshipping a mortal body, he says the Logos is God, and that Jesus' body and soul, by union with him, were changed, as formless matter can take on higher qualities. He answers the comparison with the Cretans' tomb of Zeus by quoting Callimachus, who called the Cretans liars yet admitted Zeus was born.","ideas":["Differences of belief owe much to education and fortune, which providence governs","The plainness of the Gospel writers rules out sophistical invention","Common sense forbids thinking God is corruptible matter or an image","Jesus' mortal body could exchange its qualities for ethereal and divine ones"],"terms":[{"t":"koinai ennoiai (common notions)","d":"General ideas implanted in the mind at birth, with which Origen says Christian teaching agrees."},{"t":"Logos (Word)","d":"The divine Word and Wisdom, which Origen identifies with the Son who was God from the beginning."}]},{"i":404,"j":416,"loc":"Book III, ch. 44–49","title":"Does Christianity shun the wise?","summary":"Celsus claims Christians say no educated or wise person should come to them, only the foolish, slaves, women and children. Origen replies that the faults of a few ignorant believers should not be charged to the Gospel, and shows from Scripture that wisdom is prized: David, Solomon and the queen of Sheba, Daniel, and the Gospel's use of parables and riddles to exercise hearers. Jesus explained the parables privately to disciples, and Paul ranks the word of wisdom above knowledge and faith. Paul's censure of worldly wisdom concerns those who hold only bodies to exist; he says 'not many' wise are called, not none, and requires a bishop able to teach. Origen agrees with Celsus that education is no evil but a way to virtue.","ideas":["Scripture repeatedly commends the pursuit of wisdom","The 'wisdom of the world' Paul rejects is a purely materialist wisdom","Paul says 'not many' wise are called, which implies some are","The Gospel invites both the educated and the ignorant, the latter to be healed"],"terms":[{"t":"charismata (gifts)","d":"The gifts of the Spirit listed by Paul, in which the word of wisdom stands first."},{"t":"exoteric","d":"Teaching given to outsiders, as the parables were to the crowds, as opposed to private explanation."}]},{"i":417,"j":435,"loc":"Book III, ch. 50–58","title":"Market-place tricksters and wool-workers","summary":"Celsus likens Christian teachers to market-place tricksters who avoid the wise and seek out youths, slaves and fools, and to wool-workers, leather-cutters and fullers who secretly turn children and women against fathers and teachers. Origen notes that Cynic philosophers also preach publicly, but that Christians test and privately instruct candidates, keep classes of beginners and the advanced, examine their lives, and exclude or mourn as dead those who lapse. They keep deeper teachings, the 'strong meat', for mature hearers and give 'milk' to the simple. Philosophers too invited slaves, as with Epictetus. Origen says Christians draw women and boys away from theatres, dissolute poetry and superstition, not from good teachers, and would lead those trained in philosophy higher.","ideas":["Christian admission involves testing, private instruction and graded classes","Lapsed members are mourned as dead and readmitted only after repentance","Deeper doctrines are kept for mature hearers; 'milk' is for beginners","Christians turn the young from licentious teachers, not from philosophy"],"terms":[{"t":"milk and strong meat","d":"Paul's image for elementary and advanced teaching, used by Origen for graded Christian instruction."},{"t":"catechumens","d":"Beginners under private instruction who have not yet been fully admitted to the community."}]},{"i":436,"j":453,"loc":"Book III, ch. 59–67","title":"Calling sinners: healing before initiation","summary":"Celsus contrasts the Greek mysteries, which admit only the pure, with Christians who invite sinners, fools and children, as if summoning robbers. Origen distinguishes inviting the sick to be healed from initiating the pure: the wicked are called to cure, and only the purified are admitted to the mysteries of Jesus. God the Word comes as physician to sinners and teacher to the pure; no human is without sin except Jesus, and God accepts the penitent who reforms, not mere weeping. The publican and Pharisee show why the humble penitent is preferred. Against Celsus' claim that those sinful by nature and habit cannot be reformed, Origen points to Phaedo, once in a brothel, and Polemon, once a reveller, who became philosophers.","ideas":["The Gospel heals the sick first and initiates the purified later","No one is without sin from birth; acceptance requires amended life, not lament","The penitent sinner may be preferred over the self-satisfied lesser sinner","Phaedo and Polemon show that even the most abandoned can be transformed"],"terms":[{"t":"mysteries","d":"Rites of initiation; Origen claims Christianity has its own, reserved for the purified."},{"t":"metanoia (repentance)","d":"A change of heart that, joined to a reformed life, makes the sinner acceptable to God."}]},{"i":454,"j":468,"loc":"Book III, ch. 68–74","title":"The power of the word to change natures","summary":"Origen contrasts the polished discourses of philosophy, which reformed a few, with the plain Christian preaching that converts multitudes to chastity and courage before death, a power he ascribes to the Spirit. Against Celsus' claim that nature is hard to change, he holds that all rational souls share one nature, none created evil, and that vice becomes ingrained through upbringing; will and practice can achieve virtue as tightrope-walkers achieve their feats. He explains that God can do all things consistent with being God, good and wise, and so cannot do evil at all. God does not pity mere wailing but receives those who truly repent. True wisdom does not mislead, and Christians, like legislators, provide for the unwise while preferring clever hearers.","ideas":["Apostolic preaching worked by divine power, not rhetorical skill","No rational soul is evil by creation; wickedness is acquired","God's omnipotence excludes the power to do evil","Christian teachers seek the unintelligent as a physician seeks the sick"],"terms":[{"t":"proairesis (deliberate choice)","d":"The will, which with practice Origen says can overcome deeply ingrained wickedness."},{"t":"sophia (wisdom)","d":"Defined both as knowledge of divine and human things and, from Scripture, as the breath of God's power."}]},{"i":469,"j":482,"loc":"Book III, ch. 75–81","title":"Physicians, drunkards, and vain hopes","summary":"Celsus compares the Christian teacher to a quack who keeps patients from real doctors, a drunkard accusing the sober, and a man with eye disease calling the sharp-sighted blind. Origen asks who these physicians are, and says that if Christians keep people from Epicureans, Peripatetics, Stoics with a corporeal god, or believers in transmigration, they rescue them from harm; punishment, he holds, is medicine leading to conversion. The truly drunk and blind are those who worship images and temples. The fear of punishment suits the simple, as a lawgiver gives the best laws a people can bear. Against the charge of vain hopes, he notes that Pythagoras and Plato also promised the soul an ascent, and closes Book III by challenging Celsus to name anything better than God.","ideas":["Keeping converts from Epicurean or Stoic errors is healing, not quackery","Divine punishments are corrective medicines","Idolaters, not Christians, are the drunk and the blind","If Christian hope is vain, so is Pythagorean and Platonic hope of immortality"],"terms":[{"t":"Epicurean","d":"A follower of Epicurus, who denied providence and made pleasure the good; Origen repeatedly suspects Celsus of being one."},{"t":"metempsychosis (transmigration)","d":"The doctrine that souls pass into animal bodies, which Origen rejects."}]},{"i":483,"j":497,"loc":"Book IV, ch. 1–7","title":"Why would God come down?","summary":"Opening Book IV with a prayer to Ambrosius, Origen asks for words to root out Celsus' charges and build up the truth, and turns to Celsus' attack on both Jews and Christians for expecting a God or Son of God to descend. He faults Celsus for citing no prophecies, and answers the question why God would descend: not to learn about men, but to convert the lost sheep of Israel and give the kingdom to others. Improvement by force would destroy free will, since virtue must be voluntary. God does not leave his place, since he fills heaven and earth; nor does he seek display like the newly rich, but makes himself known to save. To 'why so late', Origen replies that in every generation wisdom has entered holy souls and made them prophets.","ideas":["God does not make men good by force, since that would destroy free will","God's 'descent' is not a change of place, for he fills all things","God makes himself known to free men from misery, not from ambition","God has always sent his wisdom into holy souls in every generation"],"terms":[{"t":"autexousion (free will)","d":"The soul's power of self-determination, without which virtue would lose its essence."},{"t":"katabasis (descent)","d":"God's coming down, which Origen reads as care for human affairs, not movement in space."}]},{"i":498,"j":509,"loc":"Book IV, ch. 8–13","title":"Nations, cycles, and the consuming fire","summary":"Origen says a surpassing presence came at one time, as some prophets surpassed others, and hints at the mystery of the nations divided by number of the angels of God, with Israel as the Lord's portion and all nations later given to the Son. Christ reformed the world without the whips of the former dispensation. Against Celsus' charge that Christians use terrors like the Bacchic mysteries and misunderstood Greek tales of periodic floods and conflagrations, he argues Moses is older than those sources, and that deluge and fire come from the spread of wickedness, not planetary cycles. God 'comes down' figuratively, as a teacher condescends; God as 'consuming fire' burns away wickedness, the 'wood, hay, stubble', and refines the soul.","ideas":["The nations were allotted to angels; Israel was the Lord's own portion","Moses' antiquity means the Greeks borrowed and distorted, not the reverse","Floods and conflagrations punish wickedness rather than follow astral cycles","God's fire consumes evil works and purifies the soul like a refiner's fire"],"terms":[{"t":"ekpyrōsis (conflagration)","d":"The destruction of the world by fire, held by some Greeks to recur in cycles."},{"t":"synkatabasis (condescension)","d":"God's stooping to human affairs without change in his being."}]},{"i":510,"j":521,"loc":"Book IV, ch. 14–19","title":"God's unchangeability and the incarnation","summary":"Celsus argues that a God who came down would change from good to evil and happiness to misery, which is impossible for an immortal. Origen agrees that God is unchangeable, contrasting the atomic gods of Epicurus and the corporeal god of the Stoics, and says the Word, being in the form of God, emptied himself without sin or loss of blessedness, like a physician handling sores. The Word shows himself in different forms according to each learner's progress, as at the Transfiguration. To Celsus' dilemma that God either truly changes or deceives, Origen answers that the Word adapts its nourishment, milk or strong meat, without falsehood, and notes that Celsus himself allows deceit as medicine for sick friends.","ideas":["The Word humbled himself without passing from good to evil","The Word appears differently to beginners and the advanced","Adapting teaching to the hearer is not deception","Jesus' coming was a real manifestation, not an illusion"],"terms":[{"t":"kenōsis (self-emptying)","d":"From Philippians: the Son, in the form of God, took the form of a servant."},{"t":"oikonomia (economy)","d":"God's providential arrangement by which he deals with human affairs while remaining unchanged."}]},{"i":522,"j":533,"loc":"Book IV, ch. 20–25","title":"Purification, Jerusalem's fall, and the worms","summary":"Origen sees nothing absurd in one sent to purify a world full of wickedness, noting Plato speaks of the gods cleansing the earth with water. He rejects Celsus' claim that Moses borrowed Babel from the sons of Aloeus and Sodom from Phaethon, since Moses is older than Homer. Against Celsus' account of Christian claims about the Jews' guilt, he argues that the fall of Jerusalem within a generation of the crucifixion, and the Jews' long exile, show Jesus was divine. Celsus then mocks Jews and Christians as bats, ants, frogs and worms on a dunghill quarrelling over who sinned most. Origen replies that worth is not measured by bodily size and that reason, derived from God's reason, keeps any rational being from being likened to worms.","ideas":["Moses' narratives are older than the Greek myths Celsus says he copied","The destruction of Jerusalem is taken as divine judgment for the crucifixion","Celsus' animal satire of Jews and Christians","Reason and the seeds of virtue set humans above beasts"],"terms":[{"t":"logikon zōon (rational animal)","d":"The human being as possessed of reason, which Origen says has its beginning in God's reason."}]},{"i":534,"j":541,"loc":"Book IV, ch. 26–29","title":"Who are the worms? Piety and the angels","summary":"Origen turns Celsus' comparison back: the real worms and frogs are those who worship animals and images rather than the Maker, while those who rise from matter to God, practise temperance and justice, and treat the body as God's temple deserve no such name. Wicked members are rarely found in Christian assemblies, and the pure in heart may rightly say God has shown them things beforehand. He denies that Christians claim God abandoned the world for them alone, citing Scripture on God's love for all he made and Christ's propitiation for the whole world. Admitting angels, thrones and powers above man, he interprets 'we are next to God' as the virtuous who live by reason, for the virtue of man and God is one.","ideas":["Idolatry, not Christian belief, degrades men to the level of worms","God loves all that he has made, not Christians alone","Angels and higher powers rank above man, who may rise to their likeness","Virtue is the same in man and in God"],"terms":[{"t":"thrones, dominions, powers, principalities","d":"Ranks of heavenly beings above men, cited by Origen from Paul."}]},{"i":542,"j":549,"loc":"Book IV, ch. 30–33","title":"Image and likeness; the Jewish polity","summary":"Origen says Celsus misread Genesis: man was made in God's image but not yet in his likeness, so Christians do not claim to be wholly like God, nor that the stars serve them. He declines to mock philosophers as Celsus mocks Christians, though Paul says some Greeks knew God yet glorified him not. Against the claim that the Jews were Egyptian fugitives of no account, he praises their polity as a shadow of heavenly life: no image-makers, no harlots, judges of proven integrity, Sabbaths for study, a whole nation given to philosophy. When it declined, Providence gave all nations the religion of Jesus, which spread despite demon-stirred persecution, and the Jews were deserted after the crucifixion. He then answers Celsus' attack on Genesis, noting the power of 'God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob' in prayer and magic.","ideas":["The distinction between God's image and his likeness in Genesis","The Mosaic polity banned images and set a nation to philosophy","Christianity replaced the Jewish polity for all nations, spreading despite persecution","The names of the patriarchs carry power even in exorcism and magic"],"terms":[{"t":"image and likeness","d":"Origen holds man was made in God's image, with the likeness still to be attained."},{"t":"politeia (polity)","d":"The constitution and way of life of the Jewish nation under the law."}]},{"i":550,"j":557,"loc":"4.34–4.37","title":"Patriarchs' names, Hesiod, and the moulding of man","summary":"Origen argues that the formulae invoking the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, used even by foreigners against demons, are known only from Genesis and Hebrew tradition, so Celsus cannot call the patriarchs jugglers and deceivers. He presses Celsus to name those who supposedly dispute the names with the Jews. Celsus mocks the Jews as an uneducated people in a corner of Palestine who garbled what Hesiod and other inspired men had sung, and ridicules the creation of man, the woman from his side, and the serpent's victory. Origen replies that Moses is far older than Hesiod and the Trojan war, that Plato expelled such poets from his State, and that Genesis never speaks of God's hands; the inbreathing of life is figurative, meaning God imparted His incorruptible Spirit.","ideas":["Names that work wonders even among foreigners attest the worth of the men named","Moses is older than Hesiod, so the Greeks cannot be his source","Celsus misreads figurative phrases, turning the breath of life into inflating a skin"],"terms":[{"t":"invocation of names","d":"Formulae such as 'the God of Abraham' used against demons, which Origen treats as evidence for the patriarchs' standing."},{"t":"Theogonies","d":"Greek poems on the generation of the gods, which Origen counts as genuinely absurd stories."}]},{"i":558,"j":565,"loc":"4.38–4.40","title":"Pandora, Porus and Penia, and Adam","summary":"Celsus ridicules the woman made from Adam's rib and says the more modest Jews and Christians are ashamed and allegorize it. Origen asks why Hesiod's Pandora and her casket, quoted at length, may be read as philosophy in mythic dress while Moses may not, and whether only the Jews, so taught to rise to the uncreated God, lack a hidden meaning. Against the mockery of the serpent he sets Plato's myth in the Symposium of Porus, Penia and the birth of Eros in the garden of Zeus, noting its likeness to paradise and the serpent's plot, and suggesting Plato may have learned from Jewish thinkers in Egypt. To the charge that God could not win over even one man, Origen answers that 'Adam' means man, so Moses speaks of human nature in general, and the expulsion and coats of skins hide a doctrine of the soul losing its wings.","ideas":["Greek myths receive allegorical charity that Celsus denies to Scripture","Plato's Porus and Penia myth parallels the garden and the serpent","Adam stands for humanity as a whole; the fall signifies the soul's descent"],"terms":[{"t":"Porus and Penia","d":"Resource and Poverty, the parents of Eros in Socrates' speech in the Symposium."},{"t":"Adam","d":"Hebrew for 'man', which Origen takes as a sign that the story concerns human nature generally."}]},{"i":566,"j":579,"loc":"4.41–4.47","title":"The flood and the patriarchal narratives","summary":"Celsus calls the ark monstrous and says the flood story garbles that of Deucalion; Origen defends the ark's design, reading its measurements so that it resembled a great city, and asks how writers who gave their books to a whole nation expected them to stay hidden. Celsus then lists the begetting of children, brothers' plots, a father's grief, mothers' craft, Jacob's flocks and the patriarchs' wells; Origen identifies each episode and says these things were written as types, as Paul allegorizes Hagar and Sarah in Galatians. On Lot and his daughters, called worse than Thyestes, he cites the Stoic case of a wise man and his daughter as last survivors, and says Scripture neither praises nor condemns the act. He faults Celsus for citing Simeon, Levi and Joseph's brothers while omitting Joseph's chastity, and for turning the exodus into a flight.","ideas":["The ark was a marvel of design, not a monstrosity","Patriarchal stories are real events written for a figurative purpose","Scripture's candour about its heroes' faults supports its truth","Celsus selects the discreditable and omits the virtuous, such as Joseph"],"terms":[{"t":"things indifferent","d":"Stoic category of acts neither good nor bad in themselves, their moral character depending on intention."},{"t":"allegory","d":"Reading a narrative for a further spiritual meaning, as Paul reads the two wives of Abraham as two covenants."}]},{"i":580,"j":591,"loc":"4.48–4.53","title":"In defence of allegorical reading of Scripture","summary":"Celsus says the modest among Jews and Christians take refuge in allegory from shameful stories. Origen retorts that Greek myths of castration, devouring children and incest are far more shameful, citing Chrysippus' allegory of the Samos painting of Hera and Zeus, and says Christians will not even call God Jupiter. He argues that the founders themselves, Paul and Asaph, read Scripture allegorically, citing the ox unmuzzled, marriage as Christ and Church, and the rock that was Christ, along with the prophets' Pharaoh-dragon imagery. He notes Philo, Aristobulus and Numenius the Pythagorean, who allegorized Moses and even a story of Jesus, and defends the dialogue of Jason and Papiscus against Celsus' scorn. He closes by claiming that faith in an all-seeing God who judges thoughts best leads multitudes to virtue.","ideas":["The apostles' own practice shows Scripture was written to be read allegorically","Greek myths remain shameful even when allegorized","Philo, Aristobulus and Numenius found worthy meaning in Moses","Belief in a God who sees even thoughts reforms countless people"],"terms":[{"t":"spermatic words (logoi spermatikoi)","d":"In Chrysippus' reading, the seminal principles of God that matter receives to order the world."},{"t":"Numenius","d":"Pythagorean expounder of Plato who, Origen says, quoted and allegorized Moses and the prophets."}]},{"i":592,"j":607,"loc":"4.54–4.61","title":"Soul, body and matter: is the body God's work?","summary":"Celsus, paraphrasing the Timaeus, claims God made only immortal things, the soul is His work, and the bodies of man, bat, worm and frog are alike since their matter is the same. Origen says Celsus should have proved that bodies and plants lack a perfect intelligence behind them, and points to Genesis, where 'God made' applies to heaven, stars, animals and man. If all bodies are alike because of common matter, he argues, the sun and stars would not differ from a frog unless Celsus flees to Aristotle's fifth substance. He presents Paul's teaching on celestial and terrestrial bodies and the resurrection as a change of qualities in matter, asks whether Celsus means every soul or only the rational, and adds that bodies differ by the souls dwelling in them, as one would not bury Socrates and Anytus alike. He ends by exposing contradictions in 'no product of matter is immortal'.","ideas":["Matter is without qualities and receives whatever the Creator bestows","Bodies differ according to the soul that inhabits them","Resurrection is a change of a body's qualities to something better","Celsus' claims are asserted, not proved, and contradict themselves"],"terms":[{"t":"fifth substance (ether)","d":"The Peripatetic element of the heavens, distinct from the four, which Platonists, Stoics and Origen reject."},{"t":"qualityless matter","d":"Underlying matter, in itself without form or quality, shaped by qualities given to it."}]},{"i":608,"j":623,"loc":"4.62–4.69","title":"Is the amount of evil fixed? Cycles and free will","summary":"Celsus holds that evils have always been the same in number, cleaving to matter and mortal things, and that mortal things recur in fixed cycles. Origen cites Plato's purification of the earth by water and Chrysippus' history of the spread of prostitution to show that evil increases and decreases, as in a single person's life. He says even philosophers can hardly know the origin of evil without revelation about the devil and his angels, and that the cause of evil is each person's mind, not matter. Recurring cycles, he argues, would abolish free will and praise and blame: Socrates would be condemned again and Celsus would write his book again; the Stoic 'exactly like' version he finds unintelligible. God purifies the world by flood and fire at fixed times, not because His work was poor but to heal wickedness, as a farmer works through the seasons.","ideas":["The quantity of evil varies over time","Evil originates in the mind's choices, not in matter","Eternal recurrence would destroy free will and moral judgment","Floods and conflagrations are God's healing correction of the world"],"terms":[{"t":"apokatastasis/cycles","d":"The Stoic doctrine that after each conflagration the same order of events recurs."},{"t":"free will","d":"The power of choice that Origen says fixed cycles of recurrence would annul."}]},{"i":624,"j":633,"loc":"4.70–4.74","title":"Evil's use, God's wrath, and providence for man","summary":"Celsus says what seems evil may not be, since we do not know what benefits the whole; Origen grants that God uses the wicked for the whole, like a convict on public works, yet insists the wicked remain blameworthy, citing Paul's vessels of honour and dishonour. Celsus mocks Scripture's angry threats; Origen explains that God's word adapts itself to hearers as one speaks to children, that wrath is not passion but discipline each sinner treasures up, and that threats are like a physician's warnings. Celsus contrasts a man who destroyed the Jews with God sending His Son to suffer; Origen says the Jews' fate was the wrath they stored up and the Son suffered willingly. Celsus then denies that all was made for man, and Origen aligns himself with the Stoics who rank rational nature first.","ideas":["God turns evil to the good of the whole without excusing the evildoer","Scripture speaks of God in human terms for weaker hearers","Divine wrath is corrective discipline, not passion","Providence serves rational beings first, animals by overflow"],"terms":[{"t":"anthropopathic language","d":"Speech that ascribes human emotions to God, which Origen reads as accommodation to hearers."},{"t":"orgē (wrath)","d":"For Origen, the name for God's disciplinary judgment, brought on by the sinner himself."}]},{"i":634,"j":649,"loc":"4.75–4.82","title":"Animals, arts and the rule of reason","summary":"Celsus says (which Origen calls a sign of his Epicurean leanings) thunder and rain are not God's works, or serve plants and thorns as much as men, and that animals get food without labour. Origen calls this a denial of providence and argues God made man needy so that want would drive him to invent farming, weaving, building and navigation. To Celsus' claim that beasts hunt us with natural weapons, he answers that understanding masters even elephants and that wild beasts exercise human courage. He holds that in earliest times men were guided by divine voices and angels, so they were not mostly devoured by beasts. Celsus' bees with kings, wars and punished drones are, Origen says, an irrational nature that imitates reason so as to instruct and shame rational beings.","ideas":["Human need is providential, spurring the invention of the arts","Reason outweighs any natural weapon of beasts","First humans were helped by divine guidance","Bee and ant 'societies' are instinct, not reason"],"terms":[{"t":"Providence (pronoia)","d":"God's ordering care, which Origen says provides chiefly for rational beings."},{"t":"fortuitous concurrence of atoms","d":"The Epicurean account of the world's origin that Origen says Celsus' view implies."}]},{"i":650,"j":659,"loc":"4.83–4.87","title":"Ants, the view from heaven, and animal wisdom","summary":"Celsus praises ants for sharing loads, storing grain, burying their dead and conversing so that they possess reason. Origen warns that equating human kindness with ants' behaviour would discourage helping the burdened, and calls the ants' habits the work of Nature, not reason; he rejects the implied Platonic view that human and animal souls are of one species, since the soul is made in God's image. To Celsus' question how human acts would look from heaven, Origen replies that such an observer would see the guiding principles, rational in man, irrational in beasts. Celsus says serpents and eagles excel in protective lore such as fennel and the eagle-stone; Origen answers that each species has one fixed natural aid, whereas reason would discover many, and reads Proverbs' wise ants as figurative.","ideas":["Animal behaviour is natural instinct implanted by the Logos","Reason, shared with God, is the image of God in man","Fixed, single-purpose 'discoveries' prove the absence of reason","Proverbs' wise creatures must be read figuratively"],"terms":[{"t":"hēgemonikon (guiding principle)","d":"The ruling part of a soul, rational in humans and irrational in animals."},{"t":"Logos","d":"The divine Reason that implants in each animal its natural tendencies for self-preservation."}]},{"i":660,"j":678,"loc":"4.88–4.95","title":"Divination by birds and the work of demons","summary":"Celsus argues that birds and other animals foreknow the future, teach men augury, hold more sacred assemblies, and that elephants keep oaths, so they are nearer to God. Origen notes that the existence and mechanism of augury are disputed, mocks the idea that birds understand God better than Pythagoras or Plato, and argues that if birds foresaw the future they would avoid nets and serpents, citing Homer's omens at Aulis and of the eagle and serpent. He holds that wicked demons, fallen like Titans, enter rapacious animals to produce omens and draw men from God, which is why Moses declared divining animals unclean. He mocks the logic by which a corn-grinding slave in Homer or a sneeze would be divine, and says the true God inspires only the purest human souls, citing the law's ban on augury.","ideas":["Animals cannot foresee even their own dangers","Demons work through savage animals to deceive by omens","Moses' unclean animals match those used in divination","God reveals the future through holy prophets, not beasts"],"terms":[{"t":"augury","d":"Divination from the flights and cries of birds, which Celsus defends and Origen attributes to demons."},{"t":"unclean animals","d":"Creatures such as wolf, fox, serpent, eagle and hawk, which Origen links with divination and demons."}]},{"i":679,"j":686,"loc":"4.96–4.99","title":"Foreknowledge, animal piety, and the end of Book IV","summary":"Origen argues that foreknowledge is indifferent, found in bad physicians and pilots, so it proves no divinity; none of the irrational animals have ideas of God, and only the truly wise, like Moses, draw near Him. He finds it impious that Celsus makes serpents and hawks dearer to God than Socrates or Plato, and denies any wise man spoke of sacred animal assemblies. Elephants sometimes break faith and kill, storks feed their parents by instinct, and the phoenix, if real, shows nature's variety to lead men to the Creator. Celsus' closing claim that all was made for the whole, not man, and that God is not angry with men more than with flies, draws Origen's reply that God cares especially for each rational being, will purify the world and bring it back to Himself, and threatens men to convert them; Book IV ends with a prayer.","ideas":["Predicting the future is no sign of divinity","Apparent animal virtues are natural instinct meant to shame humans","God cares for the whole and especially for each rational being","Divine threats aim at conversion and correction"],"terms":[{"t":"phoenix","d":"The Arabian bird said to bring its dead parent to the Temple of the Sun in Egypt."}]},{"i":687,"j":697,"loc":"5.1–5.5","title":"Book V opens: descents, angels and demons","summary":"Origen addresses Ambrosius, explaining that he continues not from love of many words but to leave none of Celsus' claims unexamined, and prays for divine help. Celsus asserts that no God or son of God came or will come down, and that any descending angels would likely be demons. Origen answers that this overturns belief in Apollo, Asclepius and other gods active on earth, and convicts Celsus of Epicureanism by denying a providence that visits men; Jesus, not the oracles, is the one who came down. Angels, he says, are ministering spirits who carry prayers up and blessings down, sometimes called gods, yet prayer goes to the Supreme God through the Word alone; 'demons' always names wicked powers.","ideas":["Denying any divine descent undermines pagan oracles too","Angels carry prayers and benefits but are not to be worshipped","All prayer goes to God through the High Priest, the Word","'Demon' is always a name for evil powers"],"terms":[{"t":"angeloi (angels)","d":"Ministering spirits named from their office of carrying messages between God and men."},{"t":"daimones (demons)","d":"For Origen, wicked bodiless powers that lead men away from God."}]},{"i":698,"j":713,"loc":"5.6–5.13","title":"Heaven, angels and the worship of stars","summary":"Celsus wonders that the Jews worship heaven and its angels yet neglect the sun, moon and stars, which bring rain and heat and visibly herald God. Origen replies that the law forbids worshipping heaven, angels or the host of heaven, that idolatry of the heavens appears in Scripture only as Israel's sin, and that the Greeks themselves do not treat every part of a divine cosmos as a god. Deuteronomy assigns the heavenly bodies to the nations, while Israel and the righteous hope to shine like stars in the resurrection. The stars' light is a spark beside God, the true Light; the stars themselves pray to God through His Son, so one should pray with them to God, not to them, just as Christ referred praise to the Father. Origen adds that the heavenly bodies await liberation from vanity and praise God.","ideas":["The law forbids worship of heaven, angels and stars","A divine whole does not make each part divine","The stars pray to God and would refuse worship themselves","The righteous are destined to shine like the stars"],"terms":[{"t":"host of heaven","d":"Sun, moon and stars, which Deuteronomy says God allotted to the nations."},{"t":"true light","d":"God and His Son, in comparison with whom the stars' light is a mere spark."}]},{"i":714,"j":725,"loc":"5.14–5.19","title":"The world's conflagration and the resurrection body","summary":"Celsus mocks Christians for thinking God, like a cook, will burn the world while they alone survive and the long dead rise in the same flesh, a hope fit for worms; he says God cannot do what is shameful or against reason. Origen answers that the fire is purificatory, burning away the wood, hay and stubble of sins, citing Malachi, Ezekiel and Isaiah, and that Scripture speaks of punishment plainly to frighten simple believers into conversion. Paul's distinction between the living who will be changed and the dead raised incorruptible shows Celsus misunderstands. Neither Scripture nor the Church teaches that the same bodies rise unchanged: as Paul's seed image shows, God gives each a body as He wills, raised spiritual, incorruptible and glorious, for flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom.","ideas":["The world-fire purifies and heals rather than merely destroys","Threats of punishment suit those reformed only by fear","The resurrection body is transformed, like a plant from a seed","Corruptible and mortal must put on incorruption and immortality"],"terms":[{"t":"ekpyrōsis (conflagration)","d":"The burning of the world, which Origen reads as God's purifying fire."},{"t":"spiritual body","d":"Paul's term for the raised body, incorruptible and glorious, contrasted with the natural body sown."}]},{"i":726,"j":735,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XX–XXIV","title":"Stoic world-cycles and the Christian resurrection","summary":"Origen turns the charge of absurdity back on the philosophers: the Stoics teach that after the world-conflagration everything recurs unchanged, so that Socrates will again be born of Sophroniscus and Phaenarete and again be condemned, and Pythagoreans, Platonists and Egyptians hold similar planetary cycles. Against this he sets the Christian view that God governs according to each one's free will and improves all things. He distinguishes the Church's doctrine from Christians who deny the resurrection: following Paul's image of the sown grain, the corrupted body does not resume its former nature, but a power in it raises it in incorruption. He refuses the refuge that God can do anything, holding that God cannot do the disgraceful, and argues that what God does is never contrary to nature, though it may surpass ordinary nature. He answers Heraclitus's saying that corpses are worse than dung by defending honourable burial for the soul's sake.","ideas":["Stoic eternal recurrence makes every person and event repeat exactly","The resurrection body grows from the old one as a stalk from a grain","God can do nothing disgraceful or contrary to reason","What God does is not against nature, though it may exceed it"],"terms":[{"t":"ekpyrōsis (conflagration)","d":"The Stoic periodic burning of the world, after which, Origen reports, the same order of things returns."},{"t":"spiritual body","d":"Paul's term for the body raised in incorruption and glory, distinct from the natural body that is sown."}]},{"i":736,"j":743,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XXV–XXVIII","title":"Celsus on ancestral laws and national spirits","summary":"Origen quotes Celsus's claim that the Jews, like every nation, rightly keep their ancestral customs, since the regions of the earth were allotted from the beginning to different superintending powers, and abandoning established institutions would be impious. Origen presses him: who made this allotment, and did the ruling spirit will the Jewish laws or not? If no one allotted the regions, chance replaces providence. He then lists customs Celsus would have to call right, such as Scythian parricide, Persian incest, Tauric human sacrifice and Libyan child-sacrifice, and concludes that on Celsus's view piety would be a matter of convention, so the same act could be pious and impious. That would make every virtue relative, which Origen calls absurd, and he promises a deeper, mystical account of the original distribution of the nations.","ideas":["Celsus defends each nation's customs by appeal to allotted ruling spirits","If customs are right because local powers will them, monstrous laws become right","Piety and virtue cannot be merely relative to local law"],"terms":[{"t":"superintending spirits","d":"The powers to which, Celsus says, the regions of the earth were assigned at the beginning to govern them."}]},{"i":744,"j":753,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XXIX–XXXIII","title":"Babel, the angels of the nations, and Israel","summary":"Origen gathers Greek and Egyptian stories of gods dividing lands, then Moses's song on the Most High dividing the nations by the number of the angels and the Babel narrative. Hinting at a secret doctrine of souls entering bodies, he tells the story as veiled history: humanity spoke one divine language while it stayed in the east; those who moved away and built the tower were handed to angels of varying severity, given new languages and sent to harsher or milder lands. Those who kept the eastern speech became the Lord's portion, Israel, whose sins led to their being handed to other rulers such as the Assyrians and Babylonians. Their own ruler, mightier than the rest, now draws people out of every nation under a better law, so Christians rightly abandon local customs for Jesus's. Origen applies Isaiah's mountain of the Lord to the Church of the nations, who beat their swords into ploughshares and follow Jesus as leader.","ideas":["The Babel story read as a hidden account of souls falling away from the light","Each nation was handed to angels and lands according to its guilt","Israel was the Lord's own portion; Christ now gathers from all nations","Christians are peacemakers who have abandoned ancestral idols"],"terms":[{"t":"Shinar","d":"The plain of the tower, interpreted by Origen as gnashing of teeth, a sign of lost nourishment."},{"t":"portion of the Lord","d":"Israel, the people kept under God's own ruler rather than given to punishing angels."}]},{"i":754,"j":767,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XXXIV–XL","title":"Herodotus, native customs, and the law of nature","summary":"Celsus cites Herodotus on Ammon forbidding Libyan Egyptians to eat cows, lists the gods and food taboos of various peoples, recalls Darius's test of Greeks and Callatians, and endorses Pindar's saying that law is king of all. Origen replies that philosophers break ancestral food laws without blame, so Christians may leave images to ascend to the Creator; he mocks Ammon's rule and notes that Celsus's principle would excuse Scythian cannibalism. He distinguishes the law of nature, given by God, from the written law of cities, and says the former must be obeyed even unto death when they conflict. Through cases of an Ethiopian among Arabians, the allegory of Osiris and Isis, and Serapis's recent manufacture, he shows that inherited cults cannot settle what is divine. He explains that Christians honour virtue, wisdom and the Logos as God's Son, a second God who dwelt in Jesus's soul, and that only the true law of God is king of all.","ideas":["Celsus: every people rightly prefers its own laws, as Herodotus shows","God's natural law outranks city laws that oppose it","Serapis and animal cults show how arbitrary inherited worship is","The Son as virtue and reason containing all reason"],"terms":[{"t":"law of nature","d":"The law of which God is legislator, to be preferred whenever a city's written law opposes it."},{"t":"second God","d":"Origen's phrase for the Son as the virtue and reason containing all others, united with Jesus's soul."}]},{"i":768,"j":775,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XLI–XLIV","title":"Celsus's attack on the Jews answered","summary":"Celsus says the Jews may keep their law but have no special wisdom: Persians also worship heaven as Zeus, the name for the highest being does not matter, circumcision and food rules are found elsewhere, and their land shows they are not favoured by God. Origen praises the Mosaic constitution as withholding what is harmful, with no theatres, races or prostitution, and teaching children from infancy to seek God beyond the visible world and to believe in the soul's immortality and judgment. He commends their refusal of divination and the law freeing slaves after six years, and judges the lowest Jew wiser than philosophers who worship idols, wishing they had not killed the prophets and plotted against Jesus. Against the Persian comparison he notes the Jews' single temple, altar and high priest, and denies that heaven itself is God.","ideas":["Celsus: Jewish customs are neither unique nor holier than others","The Mosaic polity as a model of a holy, divine city","Even ordinary Jews look to the Supreme God alone","Heaven is not God, unlike the Persian identification with Zeus"],"terms":[]},{"i":776,"j":786,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XLV–XLIX","title":"Divine names, circumcision, and food laws","summary":"Against Celsus's indifference to divine names, Origen argues that names are not human conventions, as he says Aristotle held: names such as Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, Israel, Sabaoth and Adonai have power in invocations only in their original form and lose it when translated. For this reason Christians would die rather than call Zeus, Ammon or Pappaeus God, since those names belong to demons, though anyone may name God in his own tongue. Circumcision differs with its purpose, just as Epicureans, Stoics and Platonists mean different things by justice and courage. Origen suggests that an angel hostile to Israel had power over the uncircumcised, as at Zipporah's act, until Jesus was circumcised and abolished that power. Unclean foods were signs until Christ, and Christian fasting aims at mastering the body, not, like Pythagorean abstinence, at belief in transmigration.","ideas":["Names have natural power that does not survive translation","Zeus is not Sabaoth but a demon's title","The meaning of a rite depends on the intention behind it","Christian abstinence disciplines the body; Pythagorean rests on transmigration"],"terms":[{"t":"Sabaoth","d":"Hebrew divine title, rendered Lord of hosts, which Origen says keeps its power only untranslated."}]},{"i":787,"j":798,"loc":"Book V, Chapters L–LV","title":"God's favour to Israel and Celsus on angels","summary":"Origen answers Celsus's denial that the Jews were loved by God: even outsiders called God the God of the Hebrews, and Alexander honoured their high priest. That favour has passed to Christians, whom Roman efforts at extermination could not destroy. Celsus then asks whether Jesus, granted to be an angel, was the only one sent, citing sixty or seventy fallen angels chained under the earth, whose tears are hot springs, and the angels at the tomb and to Joseph. Origen rejects being called a mere angel, naming Jesus the Angel of the great counsel, and notes that no Christian claims he was the only visitor; Apelles, a follower of Marcion, held that view but rejected the Jewish Scriptures. He says Celsus muddled the book of Enoch, which the Churches do not treat as divine, mentions a reading of the sons of God in Genesis as souls drawn to bodily life, and jokes that hot springs are fresh while tears are salty.","ideas":["The divine favour once shown to the Jews now rests with Christians","Jesus is the Angel of the great counsel, not one angel among many","Celsus confuses Enoch, Genesis and the Gospels"],"terms":[{"t":"Angel of the great counsel","d":"Prophetic title Origen applies to Christ as announcer of the Father's plan for humanity."},{"t":"book of Enoch","d":"Writing on fallen angels that Origen says does not circulate in the Churches as divine."}]},{"i":799,"j":808,"loc":"Book V, Chapters LVI–LX","title":"Angels at the tomb and the shared Scriptures","summary":"Origen reconciles the Gospels' one or two angels at the tomb: one rolled away the stone, two appeared to the women. He protests that the Greeks believe apparitions reported by Chrysippus, Plutarch and Numenius while scorning Christians who would die rather than lie about God. To Celsus's taunt that the Son of God needed help to open his tomb, he replies that it was fitting for a servant to roll away the stone, and that the kingdom passed from the sinning Jews to new husbandmen. On Celsus's claim that Christians and Jews share one God, creation account and genealogy, Origen corrects his errors: Scripture says God ceased rather than rested, the brothers who plotted were Cain and Esau only, and the Exodus was no flight. Both peoples hold the books inspired, but Christians read beyond the letter, since a veil lies on Jewish hearts until they turn to the Lord.","ideas":["The Gospel accounts of one or two angels do not conflict","Christian testimony to apparitions deserves the trust given to Greek philosophers","Jews and Christians share Scripture but differ on its meaning"],"terms":[{"t":"veil","d":"Paul's image, used by Origen, for the literal reading that hides the law's meaning from those without Christ."}]},{"i":809,"j":818,"loc":"Book V, Chapters LXI–LXV","title":"Celsus's catalogue of Christian sects","summary":"Celsus lists Christians who identify their God with the Jews' God and those who oppose them, along with Valentinians, Gnostics, Ebionites, Sibyllists, Simonians, Marcellians, Harpocratians, Marcionites and others. Origen answers that philosophy and medicine also have warring schools without being discredited, and that the Church itself rejects those who teach salvation by nature or invent monstrous doctrines. He notes that Simonians do not confess Jesus at all and that he has never met several of the sects named. Against the charge that sects revile one another, he cites the duty to bless when reviled and to admonish a heretic before rejecting him, and corrects Celsus's garbled terms and his claim that all sects quote Paul, since Ebionites and Encratites reject Paul's letters. Book V closes as Celsus begins setting Christian sayings beside philosophers' better-phrased ones.","ideas":["Diversity of sects no more refutes Christianity than it refutes philosophy","Many groups Celsus names are not Christian or are unknown","Christians answer heretics by admonition, not abuse"],"terms":[{"t":"Ebionites","d":"Twofold sect that accepts Jesus but lives by the Jewish law, some affirming and some denying the virgin birth."},{"t":"Encratites","d":"A sect Origen names, with the Ebionites, as rejecting Paul's epistles."}]},{"i":819,"j":829,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters I–V","title":"Plain Scripture versus Plato on the good","summary":"Opening Book VI to Ambrosius, Origen addresses Celsus's charge that Plato said the same things better than Scripture. He defends Scripture's simple style as meant to benefit the multitude, noting that Plato is read only by the literary while Epictetus helps ordinary people, and that preaching needs power given from God. Taking Plato's letter on the highest good, which cannot be spoken but kindles suddenly like a light in the soul, he grants it is well said but applies Paul's verdict on those who knew God and did not glorify him. The same philosophers pray to Artemis at the Piraeus and offer a cock to Asclepius. Moses and the prophets knew God appears to the pure in heart, and Scripture spoke of the kindled light long before, in John, the Psalms and Isaiah, to greater effect on its readers.","ideas":["Simple speech serves the many; polished style serves few","Philosophers who knew the truth still worshipped idols","Scripture's language of divine light anticipates Plato's image"],"terms":[{"t":"chief good","d":"Plato's highest good, which his letter says cannot be expressed in words but arises in the soul."}]},{"i":830,"j":841,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters VI–XI","title":"Unwritten teaching, dialectic, and rival messiahs","summary":"Against Plato's claim that his deepest teaching could not be written for the many, Origen points to Ezekiel swallowing the scroll, Paul's unspeakable words, John's sealed thunders and Jesus's private teaching. The prophets could not have borrowed from Plato, who came later, and Scripture too commends examination by question and answer. Celsus says Plato tells no marvels; Origen recalls tales of Plato's divine birth, Pythagoras's golden thigh, and Socrates's swan dream, and cites Plato's letter naming the father of the ruler and cause. He matches Plato's name, word and image to John the Baptist, the Word made flesh, and the mark of Christ in the soul. He denies that Christians demand belief without reason, and to Celsus's picture of rival Christs he answers that Simon, Dositheus, Judas of Galilee and Theudas all came to nothing.","ideas":["Scripture also holds back what cannot be written for all","Greek lives of Plato and Pythagoras contain marvels too","Teaching is suited to each hearer: exhortation for some, demonstration for others","Only Jesus has been proclaimed Son of God across the world"],"terms":[{"t":"name, word, image, knowledge","d":"Plato's stages toward knowing a thing, which Origen maps onto John, the Word and Christ in the soul."}]},{"i":842,"j":853,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters XII–XVII","title":"Human and divine wisdom, humility, riches","summary":"Celsus claims Paul's saying that worldly wisdom is foolishness with God was borrowed from Heraclitus and Plato to lure the ignorant. Origen distinguishes human wisdom, an exercise for the soul, from divine wisdom, its end, and ranks the gifts as wisdom, then knowledge, then faith; the prophets made the distinction before the Greeks. The truly ignorant are those who pray to images, and Moses and Daniel were trained in outside learning. Christian humility, Celsus says, misreads Plato's Laws; Origen cites an older Psalm and Christ's self-emptying, defining humility as submitting to God while engaged in great things. Jesus, untaught in Greek letters, did not distort Plato on wealth into the camel and the needle; the image carries its own meaning. Origen closes with Scripture on God hidden in darkness, known fully by the Son and by those to whom he reveals the Father.","ideas":["Divine wisdom is a gift of grace beyond human wisdom","Wisdom, knowledge and faith ranked as three gifts of the Spirit","Humility means bowing under God's hand, not self-abasement","God hides in darkness and is revealed through the Son"],"terms":[{"t":"charismata (gifts)","d":"Gifts of the Spirit; Origen ranks the word of wisdom first, knowledge second and faith third."}]},{"i":854,"j":867,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters XVIII–XXIV","title":"The super-celestial place and Mithraic gates","summary":"Origen sets the prophets' sayings above Plato's words on the King of all, around whom all things exist, and alludes to the seraphim and cherubim without explaining them. Since the prophets came before Plato they borrowed nothing from him, though Plato may have learned the Phaedrus's super-celestial place from Hebrews; David spoke of the heaven of heavens, and Paul longed for unseen, eternal things. Christians hope to pass with Jesus beyond the heavens to see God face to face. Scripture names no fixed number of heavens; Jacob's ladder may point to what Plato meant by souls' passage through the planets. Celsus describes the Mithraic ladder of seven metal gates assigned to the planets, and Origen asks why he chose that cult, pointing instead to Ezekiel's gates, the heavenly Jerusalem and the camps in Numbers. He traces Celsus's diagram to the small sect of Ophites.","ideas":["The prophets anticipated Plato's ascent beyond the heavens","Christian hope: contemplating invisible things face to face","Mithraic seven gates of the planets reported by Celsus","Celsus's diagram comes from the obscure Ophites, not the Church"],"terms":[{"t":"super-celestial place","d":"Plato's region above heaven in the Phaedrus, which Origen links to the Psalms and to Paul."},{"t":"Mithras","d":"Persian mystery cult whose ladder of seven metal gates Celsus describes as a soul's planetary ascent."}]},{"i":868,"j":879,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters XXV–XXX","title":"The Ophite diagram and its archons","summary":"Origen describes the diagram he obtained: ten circles bound by one called Leviathan, soul of all things, with Behemoth beneath and a black line called Gehenna. He notes that Scripture calls Leviathan a creature God made to play, and traces Geenna to the valley of Ennom in Benjamin's lot near Jerusalem, a figure of purifying punishment that he declines to explain fully lest the many lose their fear. He rejects Celsus's account of a seal rite and of archontic angels whose ruler is the accursed god, comparing it to old slanders of infant-eating and orgies. The Ophites, who honour the serpent and curse Jesus, are not Christians, and Christians worship the God of Moses, though they deny the raising of the same flesh. He sets out the seven animal-faced archons, Michael lion-like to Onoel ass-like, to show he knows the matter better than Celsus.","ideas":["The diagram's Leviathan and Behemoth misuse Scripture","Geenna as purifying punishment, not openly taught to all","Ophites curse Jesus and cannot be counted Christians","Celsus attributes heretics' views to the Church to stir persecution"],"terms":[{"t":"Geenna (Gehenna)","d":"Place of punishment, traced by Origen to the valley of Ennom and read as purification."},{"t":"Ophites","d":"Sect named from the serpent, which praises the serpent's advice in Eden and curses Jesus."},{"t":"archontics","d":"The ruling powers of the Ophite scheme, set against angels of light."}]},{"i":880,"j":891,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters XXXI–XXXVI","title":"Gate prayers and Celsus's heap of phrases","summary":"Origen reproduces the formulae the sorcerers recite while passing the gates of the ruling powers, Ialdabaoth, Iao, Sabaoth, Astaphaeus, Aloaeus and Horaeus, each ending with a request for grace. He explains that he records them so no deceiver can claim greater knowledge, and that their makers took some names from magic and others from Scripture, wrongly treating names of the one God as separate gods. Celsus then strings together phrases such as circles upon circles, a power from Prunicos, a heaven slain, and the tree of life, mocking that a carpenter's death on a cross produced talk of a tree. Origen sorts the phrases: some come from the Ophites or Valentinians, some from Ecclesiastes or the Psalms, and some Celsus invented. He reads the death of death and the gates of righteousness from Scripture, and observes that the tree of life is in Moses and no Church Gospel calls Jesus a carpenter.","ideas":["The gate formulae belong to deluders, not to Christians","Iao, Sabaoth, Adonai and Eloi all name one God","Celsus mixes Greek, barbarian and heretical phrases","Tree of life: death came through a tree, life through a tree"],"terms":[{"t":"Ialdabaoth","d":"First ruler addressed in the gate prayers, a name Origen says was drawn from magic."},{"t":"Prunicos","d":"Valentinian name for a kind of wisdom, which Celsus garbles into a virgin power."}]},{"i":892,"j":904,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters XXXVII–XLII","title":"Diagram inscriptions, magic, and the Satan charge","summary":"Origen dismisses Celsus's mockery that a different death would have produced a precipice of life or holy leather, saying such fables fit the heretics, not the Church. He describes the diagram's further circles inscribed Father and Son, Love, Life, Knowledge and Understanding, which Christians also disapprove. He doubts Herodotus's equation of Greek and Scythian gods, denies any Christian use of spells or amulets, rejects Celsus's tale of presbyters with demon-books as slander like the infant-eating charge, and says those who pray continually are protected from magic. Celsus then charges that Christians, misreading ancient riddles, invented Satan as God's adversary, setting this against Heraclitus's war, Pherecydes's battle of Kronos and Ophioneus, the Titans and Giants, the Egyptian Typhon myth, and Homer's verses on Zeus binding Hera. He calls it absurd that the Son of God should be punished by the devil, and the range ends there.","ideas":["Christians reject both the diagram and its magical claims","Names of gods differ in meaning between peoples, not just in sound","Prayer to God guards against magic and demons","Celsus: Satan is a corrupted copy of myths of divine war"],"terms":[{"t":"Satan","d":"Hebrew name for the devil, God's adversary, whom Celsus says Christians invented from misread myths."},{"t":"Ophioneus","d":"In Pherecydes's myth, leader of the army opposed to Kronos in the battle for heaven."}]},{"i":905,"j":916,"loc":"Book VI, chapters 43–48","title":"Satan, Antichrist, and the Son of God","summary":"Origen argues that Moses, older than Heraclitus, Pherecydes and Homer, already spoke of the wicked one fallen from heaven: the serpent of Paradise, the destroying angel, Azazel, the sons of Belial, and the adversary in Job. Satan means adversary, and the one who first lost his wings is most properly so called; God places such evil in the universe as a training-school of virtue. Against Celsus's scorn for Antichrist he sets out two extremes among men, the perfection of virtue in Jesus and its opposite in Antichrist, quoting Paul on the man of sin and Daniel's bold-faced king. He then answers Celsus's claim that the title Son of God was borrowed from ancient talk of the world as God's child, citing Plato's letters and arguing that the soul of Jesus is made one with the Word, as the Church is the body the Word animates.","ideas":["Moses' scriptures precede the Greek myths of a fallen power","Evil is permitted as a testing ground for rational souls","Christ and Antichrist as the two extremes of virtue and vice","The soul of Jesus united with the Word, as one spirit with the Lord"],"terms":[{"t":"Satanas","d":"Hebrew name meaning adversary, applied to anyone who opposes virtue but most properly to the first to fall."},{"t":"Antichrist","d":"The man foretold by Daniel and Paul who embodies the extreme of wickedness, aided by lying wonders."}]},{"i":917,"j":926,"loc":"Book VI, chapters 49–53","title":"The Mosaic cosmogony and the heretics' two gods","summary":"Celsus calls the Mosaic cosmogony and the creation of man extremely silly without argument; Origen refers readers to his work on Genesis and insists that details such as the cherubim and flaming sword were not written in the spirit of comedy. He turns the charge of sheer nonsense back on Celsus and notes that the six days are complicated by the phrase about the day God made heaven and earth. Celsus's jibes about the Creator borrowing light, an accursed god, and a spirit lent and demanded back are aimed at heretical views, which Origen also rejects. Celsus then confuses Marcion's teaching with Origen's own arguments against Marcion, and asks why God created evil and cannot persuade men; Origen observes that the same objection falls on anyone, Celsus included, who affirms providence.","ideas":["Mere assertions of absurdity need no refutation","The creation days are not to be read as simple literal days","Celsus mixes up heretical sects and attacks views Christians also reject","The problem of evil equally binds believers in providence"],"terms":[{"t":"Marcion","d":"Heretic who set a good god against the Creator; Celsus quotes arguments made against him as if Christian doctrine."}]},{"i":927,"j":940,"loc":"Book VI, chapters 54–60","title":"Whether God creates evil or repents","summary":"Origen distinguishes evil properly so called, which is vice and its acts, from bodily and external evils that Scripture calls evil improperly. God did not create wickedness, or judgment would be meaningless; some evils follow from his chief works as shavings and sawdust follow a carpenter's. Bodily sufferings God may send for correction, as fathers and surgeons do. Persuasion requires the consent of the one persuaded, so failure to persuade is no defect in God. Celsus misquotes Genesis on God repenting; the flood purified the earth and sent souls, which are immortal, out of the earthly world rather than out of existence. Celsus's repeated jibe about days before the sun is met by the claim that the Word was the immediate maker, commanded by the Father.","ideas":["Only vice is evil in the proper sense","Painful external evils can be remedial discipline","Persuasion is reciprocal and depends on the hearer's will","The flood as purification, not annihilation of souls"],"terms":[{"t":"evil properly and improperly so called","d":"Origen's distinction between moral wickedness and the bodily or external misfortunes loosely called evils."}]},{"i":941,"j":950,"loc":"Book VI, chapters 61–65","title":"God's rest, voice, image and namelessness","summary":"Celsus mocks God resting like a weary workman; Origen says Scripture says God ceased, and the Sabbath is the age-long rest in which the righteous keep festival with him. God neither tires nor has hands, mouth or voice literally; such words are figurative, and God is unchanging. Man is made after the image of God in the inner man, not the body, while the firstborn Word is God's image itself. God partakes of no form, colour, motion or even substance, being rather partaken of; Origen raises whether he is beyond substance. Of Celsus's claims that God is unreachable by word and unnameable, Origen grants them of human speech but not of the divine Word, who reveals the Father.","ideas":["Bodily language about God is figurative","The image of God lies in the renewable inner man","God is participated in rather than participating, perhaps beyond substance","God is inexpressible by human words but reached through the Word"],"terms":[{"t":"after the image of God","d":"Status of man's rational soul, distinct from the Son, who is God's image itself."},{"t":"ousia (substance)","d":"Origen leaves open whether God is substance or beyond it, communicating it to others."}]},{"i":951,"j":960,"loc":"Book VI, chapters 66–70","title":"Knowing God through the incarnate Word","summary":"Celsus imagines a Christian asking how to know God and complaining of darkness, and says those led into light are dazzled. Origen answers that the darkness belongs to idol-worshippers and to Celsus, while Christians know God through the Word, the way, truth and life. The Word became flesh for those who had become flesh, then raises them to see his glory, as at the Transfiguration with Moses and Elijah. Against Celsus's version that God, being hard to see, put his Spirit in a body, Origen replies that the Son too is hard to see and that God is seen by the pure heart. God as spirit or fire is not a body; Scripture uses such terms for intelligible beings, and God is to be worshipped in spirit and truth, not with fleshly sacrifices.","ideas":["The Word reveals the Father to those who seek","Incarnation as a first stage leading to the Word's higher form","God and the Spirit are incorporeal; fire and spirit are figures","Letter and spirit as sensible and intelligible senses of Scripture"],"terms":[{"t":"pneuma (spirit)","d":"Scripture's name for intelligible, non-sensible reality, not a material breath."}]},{"i":961,"j":968,"loc":"Book VI, chapters 71–74","title":"Stoic spirit, virgin birth, and Marcion's two sons","summary":"Celsus equates Christian talk of God as spirit with the Stoic diffused corporeal spirit; Origen replies that providence contains things as a divine power, not as a body, and that incorporeal souls and angels are not destroyed by fire. Celsus's inference that the Son, as spirit, cannot be immortal, and that God gave up his spirit, rests on views no Christian holds. He mocks breathing the Spirit into a woman's womb; Origen calls the virgin birth pure, compares sunlight unpolluted by filth, and argues that a body formed without birth would not have shown its origin, like honey or wine whose source only experience reveals. Celsus's burlesque of two sons of God fighting like quails is dismissed as buffoonery in place of argument.","ideas":["Divine providence pervades all things incorporeally","The divine is not defiled by contact with the body","Appearances do not reveal how a thing came to be","Celsus resorts to ridicule for lack of weighty argument"],"terms":[{"t":"things indifferent","d":"Stoic category Origen says Celsus ignores when he calls birth a pollution of the divine."}]},{"i":969,"j":982,"loc":"Book VI, chapters 75–81","title":"Jesus' appearance and his mission to the Jews","summary":"Celsus argues that a body indwelt by a divine spirit would surpass others, yet Jesus was said to be small, ugly and ignoble. Origen notes that Isaiah foretold his lack of comeliness, while Psalm 45 speaks of his beauty and the Transfiguration shows his glory; his body appeared to each according to capacity. Mystically, the Word lacks form for those below but is transfigured for those who climb the mountain, and his garments are the words of Scripture. Against Celsus's comic picture of Jupiter waking and sending Mercury, Origen says the Word has always benefited men and came to the Jews, who knew one God and the prophets, so the gospel could spread from one corner. Many christs exist through him; Celsus's praise of Chaldeans, Magi, Egyptians and Persians as inspired is inconsistent, and Book VI closes.","ideas":["Jesus' body appeared differently according to the beholder","The Transfiguration as an image of Scripture's higher sense","Why the Word appeared first in Judea","Believers are anointed as many christs from the one Christ"],"terms":[{"t":"Transfiguration","d":"Jesus' glorified form on the mountain, read by Origen as the Word revealed to advanced souls."}]},{"i":983,"j":998,"loc":"Book VII, chapters 1–7","title":"Pagan oracles against the Hebrew prophets","summary":"Opening Book VII with a prayer, Origen turns to Celsus's attack on the argument from prophecy. Celsus complains that Christians ignore the Pythian, Dodona, Clarus, Branchidae and Ammon oracles while revering Judean sayings. Origen notes Aristotelians and Epicureans doubted oracles, and argues that even if real, they come from demons: the Pythia is entered by the spirit in an indecent way and driven into frenzy, unlike true inspiration, which clarifies the mind. Demons crave blood and sacrificial smoke; Apollo chose an ordinary woman rather than a wise man, and his praise of Socrates beside Sophocles and Euripides is faint. The Hebrew prophets, by contrast, were men of austere and fearless life, from Moses and Isaiah to Daniel, whose prophecies concerned Christ.","ideas":["Oracular frenzy marks a demonic, not divine, spirit","Demons are drawn by sacrifices rather than virtue","Prophets' holy lives fit them for the Divine Spirit","Christians cast out demons by simple prayer"],"terms":[{"t":"Pythia","d":"Apollo's prophetess at Delphi, whose ecstatic possession Origen takes as evidence of a demon."}]},{"i":999,"j":1010,"loc":"Book VII, chapters 8–13","title":"Celsus's Phoenician prophets and obscure sayings","summary":"Origen rejects Celsus's suggestion that the Judean prophecies may never have been spoken and that they resemble current Phoenician and Palestinian prophesying; since Christ no prophets have arisen among the Jews. Celsus describes wandering men claiming to be God or the Son of God, threatening fire and uttering meaningless words. Origen answers that the prophets spoke plainly on conduct and veiled deeper matters in enigmas for diligent seekers, which he has explained in his commentaries; Celsus gives neither the texts nor the names of the men he claims to have heard. Nor does Celsus cite passages ascribing shameful acts to God. On the gall and vinegar, Origen says the wicked still offer Christ the gall of their evil, which he tastes but does not drink.","ideas":["Prophecy was recorded so later readers could verify it","Obscurity in prophecy invites earnest inquiry","Celsus offers charges without citations","The Spirit's signs diminished after the apostolic age"],"terms":[]},{"i":1011,"j":1018,"loc":"Book VII, chapters 14–17","title":"Could prophecy require God to suffer?","summary":"Celsus argues that even if prophets foretold God becoming a slave, sick or dead, such base things must not be believed of God; what matters is whether the thing is worthy of God. Origen says an honest critic would show the prophecies unfulfilled, and he analyses Celsus's hypothesis with the Stoic theorem of two propositions, like the puzzle of knowing one is dead, concluding the prophets never foretold such things of God. The prophecies speak of a man who suffers, and Jesus called himself a man; no Christian says the Life or the Resurrection died. What was foretold is worthy of God: the Word dwelling in a holy human soul, while the man, perfect in wisdom, suffered for all and struck the first blow against the devil's dominion.","ideas":["Celsus's hypothesis refutes itself by a two-premise argument","It is the man Jesus, not God the Word, who died","Christ's death began the overthrow of demonic rule"],"terms":[{"t":"theorem of two propositions","d":"Stoic argument form: if A then B, and if A then not-B, so not A."}]},{"i":1019,"j":1028,"loc":"Book VII, chapters 18–22","title":"Moses' law and Jesus' teaching reconciled","summary":"Celsus asks whether Moses or Jesus taught falsely, since Moses promised wealth, dominion and slaughter of enemies, while Jesus forbade riches and taught turning the other cheek. Origen says Celsus reads the law only literally: the righteous are often poor, no Israelite lent to many nations, and the people would not have kept a law whose promises plainly failed. The law has a literal and a spiritual sense, as Ezekiel's good and not-good statutes and Paul's letter and spirit show. Riches mean wealth of speech and knowledge, as Paul lent the gospel to the nations. Slaying enemies means destroying the flesh's lusts each morning, and dashing Babylon's little ones means crushing sinful thoughts at birth against the rock of reason.","ideas":["Literal promises of wealth are contradicted by experience","The law's twofold sense, literal and spiritual","Wars against enemies as the soul's fight with vices","Psalm 137 allegorized as killing evil thoughts early"],"terms":[{"t":"letter and spirit","d":"The literal sense that kills and the spiritual sense that gives life, in Paul's phrase."}]},{"i":1029,"j":1038,"loc":"Book VII, chapters 23–27","title":"Riches, glory, the cheek, and two polities","summary":"Origen shows that Jesus' sayings on the rich, on rulers and on worldly wisdom do not conflict with the law's promises read spiritually. The Old Testament also condemns earthly glory, and the law's promised food is food of the soul, while Jesus' teaching against anxiety can be taken simply. Lamentations already says a man gives his cheek to the smiter, so the God of the law and the gospel agree. Origen grants a real difference of constitutions: the Mosaic law suited a nation with its own land and wars, while Christians under Rome cannot execute or make war; providence ended the Jewish state and temple and spreads the gospel despite persecution. He dismisses Celsus's charge that Christians think God has a body.","ideas":["Old and New Testaments agree on glory and non-retaliation","Mosaic civil law fitted a sovereign Jewish nation","Providence ended the temple and spread Christianity","Scripture teaches that God is incorporeal"],"terms":[]},{"i":1039,"j":1046,"loc":"Book VII, chapters 28–31","title":"The better land and Plato's Phaedo","summary":"Celsus says the Christian hope of a better land is borrowed from the divine men of old, Homer's Elysian plain and Plato's pure earth in the pure heaven of the Phaedo. Origen counters that Moses, older than Greek letters, promised a good and large land flowing with milk and honey, which cannot be Judea, since the whole earth was cursed through Adam. Judea and Jerusalem are shadows of the heavenly Jerusalem, as Hebrews and the Psalms on the meek inheriting the earth show. He suggests Plato's idea that precious stones reflect those of the better land came from Isaiah, and that the Greeks borrowed from the prophets. Earthly things have truer counterparts: a true light, another heaven, a Sun of righteousness.","ideas":["The promised land is heavenly, not Judea","Greek visions of the blessed land derive from the prophets","Earthly realities are shadows of truer ones"],"terms":[{"t":"pure land","d":"The good land in the pure heaven, identified by Origen with the heavenly Jerusalem."}]},{"i":1047,"j":1054,"loc":"Book VII, chapters 32–35","title":"Resurrection and the spiritual vision of God","summary":"Origen defends the resurrection, which Celsus learned from ignorant people, as unrelated to metempsychosis: the soul always needs a body fitted to its place, and the earthly tent is clothed upon with an incorruptible house, the corruptible putting on incorruption. God is not seen with bodily eyes but by the mind made in his image and the pure heart, which God must create. God is in no place, and Scripture's eyes, ears and hands are figures for a diviner sense. Celsus sends Christians to the oracles of Trophonius, Amphiaraus and Mopsus to see gods in human form; Origen calls them demons fed on sacrifice, and asks how a mere phantom, as Celsus thinks the risen Christ, could transform lives and drive out demons worldwide.","ideas":["The soul exchanges or puts on bodies suited to its condition","Only the pure heart sees God","Scripture speaks of a divine sense beyond bodily senses","The risen Christ's effects disprove the phantom charge"],"terms":[{"t":"metempsychosis","d":"Transmigration of souls, which Origen denies is the source of the resurrection doctrine."}]},{"i":1055,"j":1064,"loc":"Book VII, chapters 36–40","title":"Senses, the mind's eye, and misplaced charges","summary":"Celsus puts in Christian mouths the question how God can be known except by the senses, and urges them to turn the eye of the mind upward and shun jugglers, rather than worship a dead man. Origen faults his use of personification, contrasting Homer's consistent characters with Euripides' philosophizing slaves: neither simple believers nor learned Christians would speak so, since Scripture teaches that invisible things are known through things made, with the senses only a starting point. God is pure intelligence or beyond it, known by the mind in his image. The two eyes are already in Genesis, where sin opened the eyes of sense and closed the mind's. Celsus's charges about animal-shaped doorkeepers fit the Ophians, who never die for their beliefs.","ideas":["Celsus misuses personification by inventing Christian speech","Sense perception is a first step toward intelligible knowledge","Genesis already knows the eye of sense and eye of mind","Celsus confuses Christians with the Ophians"],"terms":[{"t":"prosopopoeia (personification)","d":"Putting words into an imagined speaker's mouth, proper only when true to that speaker's character."},{"t":"Ophians","d":"Sect that renounced Jesus and memorized names of animal-formed heavenly doorkeepers."}]},{"i":1065,"j":1072,"loc":"Book VII, chapters 41–44","title":"Plato's hard-to-find Maker and divine grace","summary":"Celsus offers inspired poets, wise men and philosophers as guides without naming them; Origen asks whether any of them lead better lives than Christ's followers, and praises the Word for giving theology to the learned and help to the simple. Celsus quotes Plato's Timaeus that the Maker and Father is hard to find and impossible to declare to all, and describes approaching the unspeakable First by synthesis, analysis and analogy. Origen admires the words but notes Plato did not call God unspeakable, and the Word made flesh revealed God to all. Human nature cannot know God without his grace; God chose the foolish to shame philosophers who still worship images, while a simple Christian prays beyond the heavens.","ideas":["Celsus's guides are unnamed and unproven","The incarnate Word makes God known to all, not few","Knowledge of God requires divine grace","Philosophers' idol worship belies their theology"],"terms":[{"t":"synthesis, analysis, analogy","d":"Celsus's three methods for conveying some notion of the unspeakable First Being."}]},{"i":1073,"j":1080,"loc":"Book VII, chapters 45–48","title":"Being and becoming, and Christian purity","summary":"Celsus sets out his teaching: intelligible being is known by reason and bears truth, visible becoming by the eyes and breeds error, and as the sun makes vision possible, an ineffable cause makes intellectual knowledge possible. He tells Christians, if they cannot grasp this, to keep silent as men lame in mind. Origen accepts sound reasoning from any source and says Christians do distinguish being from becoming, using visible things as steps to God's eternal power. Yet Romans shows that philosophers who knew such truths worshipped images and fell into shamelessness. By contrast the despised simple Christians keep virginity without hemlock, which the Athenian hierophant needed, and without seeking honours or reward.","ideas":["Celsus's Platonic sun analogy for the highest cause","Christians also rise from visible things to invisible","Knowledge without right worship led philosophers astray","Christian virginity contrasted with pagan priestly purity"],"terms":[{"t":"ousia and genesis (being and becoming)","d":"Celsus's Platonic division between intelligible reality and the visible world of change."}]},{"i":1081,"j":1098,"loc":"Book VII, Chapters 49–57","title":"Better heroes than Jesus: Orpheus, Anaxarchus, Epictetus","summary":"Origen closes his comparison of Greek and scriptural teaching, insisting that the prophets and apostles said the same truths better, and that even simple believers live purer lives than self-styled sages. He answers Celsus on the soul's entanglement in generation with scriptural texts on sin from birth and the vanity of earthly life. Celsus then mocks Christians for not choosing a nobler object of worship: Hercules, Aesculapius, Orpheus, Anaxarchus, Epictetus, the Sibyl, Jonah or Daniel. Origen rejects each in turn, arguing that Jesus' silence under scourging outweighs any Greek saying, that his prayer in Gethsemane shows submission rather than cowardice, and that Jonah and Daniel cannot be ranked above him.","ideas":["Truth held by Greeks was stated more fully by the prophets and apostles","Earthly life is called vanity and a place of affliction compared with the life to come","Jesus' silent endurance under torture surpasses the sayings of Anaxarchus and Epictetus","Celsus slanders Jesus' life as infamous without naming a single deed"],"terms":[{"t":"genesis (becoming)","d":"Coming into being through birth, which Celsus links with error and Origen with inborn sin."}]},{"i":1099,"j":1106,"loc":"Book VII, Chapters 58–61","title":"Turning the other cheek and Plato's Crito","summary":"Celsus claims the precept to turn the other cheek is a coarse copy of Socrates' argument in the Crito that one must never return injustice for injustice. Origen replies that a sound doctrine keeps its value whoever first stated it, and that Jewish writings are older than Greek ones in any case. He compares truths to wholesome food: Plato prepares it for a refined few, while the prophets and apostles season it for the multitude and so do more good. Like a Greek learning Egyptian to help Egyptians, the divine Word adapted itself to simple hearers while hiding deeper meanings for those who study.","ideas":["The same truth may be found among Greeks, Jews and Christians without losing worth","Plain speech that helps many is better than elegant speech that helps few","Scripture accommodates the simple but holds deeper truths for careful students","Jesus' precept is more effective in practice than the Crito's argument"],"terms":[{"t":"accommodation","d":"Origen's view that the divine Word adjusts its language to the capacity of ordinary hearers."}]},{"i":1107,"j":1124,"loc":"Book VII, Chapters 62–70","title":"Rejecting images, temples and the service of demons","summary":"Celsus likens Christian refusal of temples, altars and images to that of Scythians, Libyan nomads, Seres and Persians, cites Heraclitus, and charges Christians with contradiction since they call man God's image. Origen answers that the same act may spring from different motives, illustrating with Stoic, Epicurean and ordinary reasons for avoiding adultery; Christians refuse images because of God's commands, not as barbarians do. He holds that God's image lies in the rational soul, that images and their dedications serve demons, and that Christians drive demons out rather than serve them. Against Celsus' argument that all things, including demons, follow God's will and deserve worship, Origen distinguishes what God orders from what he permits and says demons are beings that fell from God. He postpones the question of serving many masters to Book VIII.","ideas":["One outward act differs morally according to the principle behind it","The likeness to God is in the reasonable soul, not in bodily form","Sins happen by God's permission, not by his ordering","Demons are fallen beings, so worshippers of God may not serve them"],"terms":[{"t":"daimones (demons)","d":"For Origen, spirits that fell away from God and receive the worship given to pagan gods."},{"t":"providence","d":"God's government of all things, which Origen says covers what is just, while evil is merely permitted."}]},{"i":1125,"j":1141,"loc":"Book VIII, Chapters 1–8","title":"No one can serve two masters","summary":"Opening Book VIII with a prayer, Origen takes up Celsus' charge that the saying about not serving two masters is the language of sedition, and that God, unlike a human master, loses nothing if many gods are served. Origen explains scriptural usage of gods and lords: there are gods in name and true gods, and one God and one Lord through whom believers rise above all others. Refusing other masters harms not God but ourselves, for we would lose our portion in him; the Lacedaemonian envoys who would not bow to the Persian king are an example. He presses Celsus on what injury heroes and demons could suffer, citing Socrates that the worse cannot harm the better, and concludes that serving God benefits the servant.","ideas":["Scripture distinguishes gods and lords in name from the one God and one Lord","Christians separate from those outside God's covenants as citizens of heaven","Exclusive service guards the worshipper's good, not God's interest","Ambassadors for Christ must bow to no earthly ruler"],"terms":[{"t":"stasis (sedition)","d":"Faction or revolt, which Celsus says the 'one master' teaching expresses."}]},{"i":1142,"j":1157,"loc":"Book VIII, Chapters 9–16","title":"Worship of the Son and the Heavenly Dialogue","summary":"Celsus allows honour only to those whom God permits to be honoured; Origen asks for proof that pagan gods, or Hadrian's favourite Antinous, received such permission, while prophecy and miracles show that God gave honour to Jesus. Honouring God and the Son consists in an upright life. Against the claim that Christians worship God's servant beside God, Origen says Father and Son are two persons but one in thought and will, that the Son existed before Abraham, and that prayers go to the Father through the Son as high priest. He denies that the Son is above the Father, citing 'the Father is greater than I', and dismisses a quoted Heavenly Dialogue as the work of obscure heretics who set another god over the Creator.","ideas":["Honour to Jesus is warranted by prophecy and divine power, unlike honour to Antinous","Father and Son are two subsistences one in harmony and will","Prayer is offered to God through the Son as high priest","Celsus attributes heretical sayings to all Christians"],"terms":[{"t":"hypostasis (subsistence)","d":"A distinct existent; Origen calls Father and Son two in this sense but one in will."}]},{"i":1158,"j":1179,"loc":"Book VIII, Chapters 17–27","title":"True altars, statues, temples and feasts","summary":"To the charge that refusing altars, statues and temples marks a secret society, Origen answers that the good man's spirit is an altar, prayers are its incense, the virtues are statues fashioned by the Word, and believers' bodies, above all Christ's body, are God's temple built of living stones. He then rejects Celsus' call to join public feasts: for the perfect Christian every day is the Lord's day, Preparation, Passover and Pentecost, while set days serve weaker believers. Following Paul on idol offerings, he says those who share the table of demons cannot share the Lord's table. Demons do not belong to God, unlike good angels and men, and they are not to be believed, propitiated or prayed to; God's angels protect Christians from them.","ideas":["Virtues formed in the soul are statues surpassing those of Pheidias","The whole life of the mature Christian is a continuous festival","Eating idol offerings joins one to the table of demons","Scripture calls good angels God's, but demons are all wicked"],"terms":[{"t":"eidōlothyta (idol offerings)","d":"Food sacrificed to idols, which Origen with Paul says is offered to demons."}]},{"i":1180,"j":1199,"loc":"Book VIII, Chapters 28–37","title":"Food, first-fruits and the powers of nature","summary":"Celsus argues that if Christians avoid sacrificial meat to escape eating with demons, they should avoid all food, since bread, wine, water and air come from demons set over nature. Origen explains the apostolic ban on idol offerings, strangled things and blood, which demons feed on, and says Christians abstain at times for reasons other than Pythagoras', rejecting transmigration. The invisible husbandmen of nature are angels, he holds, while demons act only as executioners of famine and plague under divine judgment. So believers give thanks and first-fruits to the Creator, eat the bread that becomes a sacred body by prayer, and rely on guardian angels; they fear no satraps of the air and pray in their own tongues, not by barbarous names.","ideas":["Angels, not demons, oversee the fruits of the earth and the air","Demons serve as public executioners of divine punishments","Christians rejoice in angelic helpers who pray alongside them","God hears prayer in every language"],"terms":[{"t":"Eucharist","d":"The bread over which thanks is given, called a sacred body that sanctifies sincere partakers."},{"t":"metensōmatōsis (transmigration)","d":"The passing of souls into other bodies, including animals, which Origen denies."}]},{"i":1200,"j":1213,"loc":"Book VIII, Chapters 38–44","title":"Reviling the gods, divine punishment and martyrs","summary":"Origen denies that Christians boast of beating statues unpunished; Scripture forbids reviling, and Christians mock only those who worship images. He rejects Celsus' taunt that the Son of God is a demon who took no vengeance, and contrasts oracles about inherited guilt with Ezekiel's teaching that each dies for his own sin. To the claim that the crucifiers went unpunished he points to the siege and ruin of Jerusalem, and calls the rise of the Church something new, like the grain of wheat that dies and bears fruit. Demons persecute Christians because Christians expel them, but martyrs break their power, and a Christian who flees does so by his Master's command.","ideas":["Christians are forbidden to revile even gods or demons","Each person is punished for his own sin, not his ancestors'","Jerusalem's fall followed the shedding of Jesus' blood","Martyrs' deaths defeat the demons and bring periods of peace"],"terms":[]},{"i":1214,"j":1229,"loc":"Book VIII, Chapters 45–52","title":"Oracles, eternal punishment and the body","summary":"Celsus lists the cures, cities and punishments credited to oracles; Origen asks why these should be believed and Scripture's wonders called fables, and sets the prophets' deeds against Apollo's bribable priestess. Celsus likens Christian teaching on eternal punishment to the mystery-priests' threats; Origen says the truer doctrine is the one that changes how people live. Against the charge of valuing the body yet exposing it to torture, he says the soul is above the body, and that suffering for piety honours the body. He criticizes Celsus for urging belief in rewards and punishments while trying to make Christians abandon it, unlike Chrysippus, who argued even on premises he rejected to help people.","ideas":["Scripture's wonders are better attested and more beneficial than pagan oracles","A doctrine of punishment is proved true by the lives it reforms","The rational soul, not the body, bears the Creator's likeness","The order of the cosmos should warn all people of judgment"],"terms":[{"t":"mystagōgos (initiator)","d":"A priest who introduces people to the mysteries, whose threats Celsus compares with Christian teaching."}]},{"i":1230,"j":1247,"loc":"Book VIII, Chapters 53–61","title":"Keepers of the prison-house and Egyptian demons","summary":"Celsus suggests, citing Empedocles, that souls in bodies are entrusted to keepers of a prison-house; Origen faults his hesitancy toward Greek views while dismissing Jewish and Christian ones, and says Jesus frees the prisoners Satan has bound. Celsus offers a choice: either leave life and have no children, or honour the powers that give its goods. Origen replies that Christians leave life only when piety demands, may marry and raise children, and owe thanks to God, whose symbol is the Eucharist, and to angels only praise, not worship. To Celsus' 36 Egyptian demons of bodily parts he opposes the name of Jesus, warning that seeking demons' help is magic and that demons sell their favour to the highest bidder.","ideas":["Jesus releases souls bound in the 'prison' of earthly life","Christians may marry and share in life without serving demons","Medicine or prayer to God, not demons, is the right path to healing","Simple devotion pleases God more than curious study of demon names"],"terms":[{"t":"katalēptikē phantasia (apprehensive perception)","d":"The Stoic criterion of truth, which Origen says Celsus cannot use for his claims about Jesus."}]},{"i":1248,"j":1262,"loc":"Book VIII, Chapters 62–68","title":"Earth-spirits, kings and the fortune of the emperor","summary":"Origen seizes on Celsus' admission that oracular earth-spirits crave blood and odours, saying it confirms the Christian view, and refuses sacrifice even if health depended on it. Celsus wavers, saying we must never lose hold of God, yet urging Christians to win the favour of rulers raised up by demons. Origen answers that God's favour brings that of all good angels, that Christians obey authorities but will not flatter or swear by the king's fortune, which is either nothing or a demon. They praise the sun as God's work but will not hymn Minerva, whose myths he recounts, and they reject Homer's king appointed by Saturn's son, holding that God sets up kings.","ideas":["Celsus himself concedes that oracle-givers are base earth-spirits","Having God's favour brings the help of all his angels","Swearing by the emperor's fortune means swearing by nothing or by a demon","Kings are appointed by God, not by the gods of Greek myth"],"terms":[{"t":"tychē (fortune)","d":"The emperor's fortune, by which Romans swore; Origen calls it nothing or a demon."}]},{"i":1263,"j":1278,"loc":"Book VIII, Chapters 69–76","title":"Christians, the empire and the conclusion","summary":"Celsus mocks the idea that God would fight for a Christian Rome, pointing to the landless Jews. Origen replies that a united, praying empire would overcome its enemies or need no war, since the just are the salt of the earth, and that the promises to Israel were conditional. Against Celsus' doubt that all nations could share one law, he holds that the Word will one day prevail over every rational soul and heal all evil, citing Zephaniah. Christians help the king by prayer as a priestly army rather than by bearing arms, and serve in the Church instead of public office. He ends by asking Ambrosius to send Celsus' promised sequel, if written, for reply.","ideas":["Prayers of Christians aid the ruler more than soldiers do","Christians, like pagan priests, keep their hands free of blood","The Word will finally bring every rational being under one law","Church leadership is a higher civic service than public office"],"terms":[{"t":"Logos (the Word)","d":"Christ as divine Reason, whose healing Origen expects to overcome all evil in souls."}]}]});